/* walk-i18n-en.js -- the ENGLISH side for translate.html: every string on walk.html in page order, and every caption cue with its timings. Regenerate when the article changes. */
window.WALK_I18N_EN={"order": ["Save", "our", "forecasts", "About", "News", "Blog", "Contact", "CV", "Back", "Discrete", "Continuous", "scroll", "scroll!", "Walking 250 miles from NYC to DC", "A walk in support of weather and climate science.", "All opinions expressed on this site are personal.", "By Syl Foisy · 9/19/2026", "25 min", "English", "i", "All Stops", "Interview", "Video", "Crossed state line", "Food review", "Central Park", "Manhattan, NY", "Battery Park", "Conference House Park", "Staten Island, NY", "Davidson's Mill Pond Park", "North Brunswick, NJ", "Lt. John Farnkopf American Legion", "South Brunswick, NJ", "Forrestal Road", "Princeton, NJ", "Institute Pond", "Lower Trenton Bridge", "Trenton, NJ", "Holiday Inn Express", "Bensalem, PA", "Victory Brewing Company", "Philadelphia, PA", "Radnor Green Park", "Claymont, DE", "Fire Station #5", "Wilmington, DE", "University of Delaware", "Newark, DE", "Perryville Park", "Perryville, MD", "WoodSpring Suites", "Nottingham, MD", "University of Maryland", "College Park, MD", "United States Capitol", "Washington, DC", "Preface", "April 22 to May 22", "Hi, I'm Syl! I'm an Earth Science PhD candidate studying aerosols -- tiny liquid and solid bits of matter in the atmosphere -- who recently walked from NYC to DC. Why did I do this? Well, to answer that, we need to go back to April 22nd, 2026.", "On April 22nd, I received a message on my phone from Andrew Williams, whom I fondly co-hosted the opening of the Weather & Climate (WC) Livestream with in 2025 and who many have rumored to be my long-lost brother. :D Andrew noted in the WC Livestream organizing chat a few alarming facts:", "The National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) had shed over 1,000 employees since January 2025 (roughly 20% of its workforce) through layoffs, early retirement incentives, and firings", "The National Weather Service (NWS) had lost more than 500 employees since 2024, leaving at least eight of the nation's 122 forecast offices unable to operate overnight", "Roughly 400 scientists/experts working on the next National Climate Assessment had recently been dismissed", "Andrew and me co-hosting hours 0-3 of the 100 from last year's Weather & Climate Livestream... we did not plan to wear the same shirt lol!", "With these facts in mind, Andrew suggested the need for a followup to last year's 100 hour Weather and Climate Livestream, which had been seen by nearly 200,000 people, and resulted in 30,000 calls to members of Congress detailing why climate and weather science is worth funding.", "After a few organizing meetings discussing the ongoing urgency of this situation, I pitched walking from NYC to DC as a way to generate publicity, half jokingly. To my surprise, the idea generated some excitement! The organizing team graciously helped shape the idea into a physical manifestation of the Livestream's goal; instead of calling Congress, I would hand deliver real paper letters to members of Congress. To make matters more epic, a 50 hour livestream would still be planned to allow for maximum participation from the scientific community, coinciding with the final 50 hours of my walk to the Capitol Building!", "Thrilled, I spent a good deal of time preparing and imagining what such a walk could shape up to be; how to best craft a narrative that would complement delivering letters to Congress. While an amazing crew of organizers were helping shape details for the 50-hour Livestream logistics (shout-out Andrew Williams, Jonah Bloch-Johnson, Paulina Czarnecki, Stella Heflin, and Clare Singer!), walk letter collection (shout-out Virginia Schutte!), walk letter handoff strategy (shout-out Haley Crim and Marc Alessi!), and overall social media strategy (shout-out Berenize Garcia Nueva and Heather Autumn!) the walk itself was largely up to me to plan. After chatting with some of my beloved friends and family, I settled on two main objectives:", "Communicate the kinds of weather and climate research scientists actually do", "Bring awareness to what Americans stand to lose from federal budget cuts", "With these objectives set, I then began the actual planning process. First, I needed a route. Luckily, I knew just the person. Jacob LD-- one of my best friends from college, had walked across the United States in 2024. I hopped on a Zoom call with Jacob, and together we created a first draft of a route--", "Step one, done!", "The next stage was a bit more tricky. Namely, brainstorming how to communicate weather and climate science along the way. I settled on the idea to attempt to set up in-person interviews with researchers and experts who were along the walking path, knowing it was a long shot for people to say yes. I reached out to 92 scientists along my planned route, of which 11 responded yes to an in-person interview... honestly... not a bad yield! (12%). Emails were tricky, as I had to provide the time I would be arriving, which relied heavily on the assumption my walk wouldn't be delayed. To work around this, I provided a 2-3 hour time window and a link to a group chat to communicate day-of to all the experts that agreed to take part.", "Having secured the 11 interviews, I then procured 500 stickers, 25 vintage pins, and 20 embroidered hats to hand out along the walk. I figured having things to hand out would make conversing with folks a bit easier, and would be a way to thank the interviewers for their time. See below a cube full of merch images --", "❮", "❯", "Walk merch cube! Click and drag to see other sides. Note to future self: coding and debugging a rotating cube with images is actually really, really difficult. xD", "As the organizing pieces around the walk began to fall into place, some excitement grew within the organizing group, and I was lucky enough to help convince two other folks to join me on this journey. Miriam Nielsen, a climate science postdoc and legendary video creator (@zentouro), and Virginia Schutte, an award-winning freelance science communicator (@vgwschutte), volunteered to support me throughout the duration of the walk, helping with all things logistics and content. Them joining was a pivotal moment for the project. Right off the bat, Virginia made an impact, recruiting three additional interviews from folks she knew at Rowan University, bringing the grand total to 14. Miriam also made a quick impact, volunteering to bring along a full-amenities, tricked out, camping van, AKA \"The Content Bus\", as a place to create content along the way.", "Momentum, momentum, momentum!", "As the days approached, my anticipation grew, but to stay grounded, I wrote on a slip of paper my own personal goals for the walk-- I have had experience with", "social media", ", but not so much with talking to cameras (scary!!), so this was new ground for me. I wrote the words: wonder, excitement, positivity, and fun on this slip of paper, which I tucked into my wallet. I would aim to embody these words throughout the following 12 days of adventure...", "The actual slip of paper that lived in my wallet during the full duration of the walk.", "That's enough preface from me though--", "Let's walk from NYC to DC!!", "The walk began at 10:00 AM on May 23 at the Great Hill in Central Park, where roughly a dozen other weather and climate enthusiasts kindly joined me to brave the 2-3\" of rain in the forecast.", "Annika Horne, a documentary film maker, created a wonderful recap video of this sendoff, which you can", "view here", ", as we began our meander down Central Park and towards midtown Manhattan. To start things off with a bang, the walk's first official stop was at Hershey’s Chocolate World. Why a bang, do you ask? Because we each received free chocolate by walking through the door! Pro tip for anyone visiting New York. Talk about a walker's paradise. :)", "Well-fueled from the chocolate, we continued down the spine of Manhattan, making a second stop at Tashkent Supermarket for lunch, before meeting Hikaru Wakeel Hayakawa for the opening expert interview of the walk. Hikaru, the executive director of Climate Cardinals, myself, and co-organizer of the 2026 WC Livestream and walk Berenize Garcia Nueva are friends from college, so this was a really fun reunion!", "Watch interview #1 ft. Hikaru below!", "I couldn't think of a better way to start the walk than with Hikaru's interview-- serving as a reminder that science is only as useful as it is understood. Inspired by Hikaru’s interview, this website is actively being translated into five languages: Malagasy, Spanish, French, Vietnamese, and Mandarin.", "Towards the end of Hikaru's interview, the rain started to pick up. To see just how hard the rain was falling, you can click the button below to see the exact radar at this moment, and throughout the walk, courtesy of", "NOAA National Centers for Environmental Information", "data! The yellow-red colors represent heavier rain. Click \"Loop\" multiple times to change the playback speed, or view a specific timeframe using the slider!", "🌪", "Plot radar on the map", "After the interview with Hikaru, a few folks peeled off to avoid the rain, but a few more joined, as the group continued the journey down to Battery Park, the site of interview #2 with Dr. Allegra LeGrande. Allegra helps organize", "Hike the Heights", ", and is a world renowned expert in past Earth climates-- perhaps the most on-brand expert of the entire project. Watch interview #2 ft. Allegra below!", "Allegra's interview was meaningful for a number of reasons, perhaps most of all to me because it demonstrated how creative weather and climate scientists can be in their quest to study Earth's past. The fact that shells of creatures at the bottom of the ocean hold secrets about what the temperature was like up to billions of years ago is nothing short of mind blowing. After Allegra’s video, much of the group departed, with the rain picking up in intensity and the Upper Bay in sight, indicating the end of the Manhattan walking stretch. Just a handful of hardy folks continued with me across the Staten Island ferry to begin the next stretch of land.", "Staten Island brought a new vibe, with lovely sidewalks and conversation drifting toward atmospheric radiation-- the study of how sunlight travels through Earth’s atmosphere. After an hour of animated lightwave discussion, the group had dwindled to just two: myself and co-Livestream organizer Virginia Schutte. Fortunately, we found some company at Mile 15, where we were introduced to what I like to think was Howard Fox, the turkey (Who knew Staten Island had turkeys?!).", "Virginia and I chugged along until we reached a marathon's distance (26.2 miles!), and called it a day at the sight of an appealing looking restaurant called Cucumber Sushi & Salad Bar. Just before dinner, Virginia and I had our first introduction to the highly anticipated Content Bus-- which Miriam had picked up from Brooklyn and driven to meet us in. The rain had soaked through our clothes, so we were both freezing, and very grateful to be able to warm up over a nice meal. See how cold it was by clicking the temperature button below... data courtesy of", "Climate Data Online", "from NOAA's National Centers for Environmental Information!", "🌡", "Plot temperature on the map", "The next morning, the rain was somehow even heavier, and it was a solo journey for me, as Miriam and Virginia got to working on creating the walk's first content-- some short videos you can see by clicking", "here", "or", "! I was dropped off where we had made it to the day prior, and continued my trek southwest. For the final mile of Staten Island, I recorded a short summary video-- the first \"vlog\" of the trip. See below!", "This concluded the Staten Island section of the trip!", "Since there was no pedestrian friendly bridges to cross into New Jersey, I next received a ride from the Content Bus over the Eugenius Harvey Outerbridge, to be dropped off directly across the shore at the Raritan Bay Waterfront Park in South Amboy, NJ. Once in New Jersey, I continued the trek along the South Amboy roads, through Sayreville, and eventually through East Brunswick and Milltown, where I recorded a Mile 38 to 40 update below.", "A few hours later, I found myself in North Brunswick Township, where at Mile 42, it was time for the Interview #3 of the walk-- this time, at a location a bit off the walking path. Miriam and Virginia picked me up, and we drove 15 minutes east to meet Dr. Alan Robock, a Professor at Rutgers University, at Monmouth Battle Monument in Freehold, NJ. This was a convenient location halfway between the walking path and where he was residing and we were thrilled he was able to work us into his schedule. Alan is a world-leading expert in nuclear winter research and all things climate and was a thrilling interview. Watch Interview #3 ft. Alan below!", "Alan's interview was meaningful to me for a number of reasons, perhaps most of all because of how strongly he believes-- and has demonstrated-- the importance of scientists speaking out when they discover a potential danger to society. After meeting with Alan (and his amazing wife, who came along to support), I returned to the continuous footpath to complete another two miles, before calling it a day at 16 miles. What a start to the trip!", "The next morning, I got my earliest start yet (8:00 AM), and I began my traverse through the North Brunswick area of New Jersey, filled with wildlife and forest land-- the first quasi-rural portion of the trip. In this stretch, I saw people fishing, running, and enjoying the outdoors, and personally, it was really nice to take a few moments to re-set and center myself next to the flowing water of Davidson's Mill Pond Park.", "I also saw the second wildlife of the trip, following the Staten Island turkey-- watch the video below to see!", "After passing through South Brunswick Township, only Plainsboro Township remained between me and the next interview location, Princeton, NJ, 12 miles down the road. I hadn't expected any memorable moments to arise over this next stretch... however, that proved to be very false!", "After continuing a few miles down the road from Crossroads South Middle School, I was surprised to see an open door with a sign that said “Come on in!” and the smell of food... a walker’s dream. How could I not walk in? As it turns out, a Memorial Day ceremony had just ended at the Lt. John Farnkopf American Legion Post 401, and a reception was well underway. I walked in a bit hesitantly, but was quickly welcomed by a few organizers, and before I knew it, I was being provided with water bottles, snacks, and even a warm meal for the road. Inspired by the generosity of the folks, I gave out nearly 20 livestream stickers as a token of my appreciation.", "During my time at the reception, I chatted with folks about my favorite ice nucleating bacteria, and the mission of the walk I was on. Many could not believe I had started in Central Park just a few days prior! I was very grateful for this stop, and it was one of the more memorable ones on the trip. It was also an honor to pay my respects for those who had served, and to their families.", "After the Legion, I continued the journey south, where I filmed the first and only timelapse video of the trip, using a GoPro Virginia kindly lent me. In the video, you'll hear one of my Mom's fiddle tunes, which I was listening to on this section! In the beginning of the video, you can also see a point of view of me filming a", "short video on ice nucleating bacteria", "-- a video made better from the feedback I received from the folks at the Legion whom I had practiced on a few miles before (They were so kind to me!).", "The next few miles went quickly, and I soon found myself in Princeton, NJ.", "As I arrived to the final destination of the day, the Geophysical Fluid Dynamics Laboratory (GFDL), I was thrilled be greeted with the first ray of sunshine of the entire trip, which I documented in the video below.", "Upon my arrival, I was truly exhausted, as some of the early adrenaline had started to wear off. Fortunately, a federal research scientist, Dr. Nadir Jeevanjee, offered to host me and the rest of the content crew for a night, which boosted my spirits in a major way. Below, you can see an image of me editing the ice nucleating bacteria video I mentioned, while rolling out my back at Nadir's house!", "Arriving at Nadir's house was wonderful for a multitude of reasons, two of which I want to highlight here. Firstly, Dr. Andrew Williams -- lead organizer of the WC Livestream, incoming Columbia University Earth Science Professor, and person who helped inspire this entire walk -- arrived to join me for the following day's walk. Secondly, we all had an incredible daal dinner provided by Nadir and his family-- the best meal by far of the trip.", "1/5th of the walk done!!", "The morning after the best sleep so far on the trip (another shout-out to Nadir!). I returned to Forrestal Road for Interviews #4, 5, and 6 of the walk. The first was with Dr. Mitch Bushuk, a GFDL scientist who is a world renowned expert in polar climate and sea ice prediction. In the interview, Mitch eloquently breaks down what a climate model really is. Watch Interview #4 ft. Mitch below!", "Mitch's interview was something I wish I had watched before starting my PhD, as understanding a climate model can be extremely tricky-- they can be over a million lines of code! Mitch's testimony about the federal budget cuts was also extremely poignant, specifically how they are negatively impacting the quality of forecasts GFDL is able to produce. Following Mitch, interview #5 of the trip was with Dr. Tim Merlis, a GFDL scientist who is a world leading expert in the circulations of atmospheres both on Earth and on other planets throughout the Universe. Watch Interview #5 ft. Tim below!", "Tim's interview was thrilling, and was a great example in my opinion of the serendipity of research. Namely, building climate models for Earth has helped us better identify which planets throughout the universe may house life! Another highlight was how he meticulously breaks down how counter-factual scientific inquiries are made possible by employing models. With these two incredible GFDL interviews completed, the next was with Bernadette Woods Placky, a globally acclaimed meteorologist and climate expert. Watch Interview #6 ft. Bernadette below!", "Bernadette has been a hero of mine for a few years, so this interview was extra special. Additionally, I think her interview was an amazing example of how collaborative the weather forecasting process is-- you need input from observations, models, and interpretations from experienced humans working together to create the very best forecasts. This seamless collaboration that occurs behind the scenes is undoubtedly a great example of why the US has been a world leader in weather and climate science for all these years. Lastly, in honor of Bernadette's pro tip about paying attention to dew point, I've added in a feature to visualize the dew point for each and every moment of the walk below, with data again courtesy of", "💧", "Plot dew point on the map", "Pro tip: Click the \"Loop\" button a few times until you reach the 16x playback-- check out how the dew point dances over the Great Lakes!", "Following these wonderful interviews, it was time to continue my journey. Nadir, acclaimed federal research scientist and host of the content crew the previous day, was set to meet me at the Institute Pond, four miles south, for Interview #4 of the day, and #7 of the trip overall. For this stretch, Tim graciously joined to show me a scenic path on the route over. Watch this fun stretch with Tim below!", "It was incredible to have Tim join me for the section, and we made it in no time to Institute Pond for Nadir's interview.", "Upon arriving at the Institute Pond, it was exciting to see the historic Fuld Hall overlooking the water. To hear more about Fuld Hall, Nadir's interview is a must-watch. Nadir is a world renowned expert in atmospheric radiation, and part of his research was actually the basis of one of the classes I’ve taken in grad school. This expertise on radiation, coupled with his expertise on the history of weather and climate forecasting, made this one of the more memorable interviews of the entire trip. Watch Interview #7 ft. Nadir below!", "Nadir's interview was powerful for so many reasons, but one that stands out to me is just how accurate climate forecasts have historically been. Whether it's the sensitivity of Earth's temperatures to carbon dioxide, or Arctic amplification-- it's uncanny how well humans have been able to predict future climates. I would be remiss to also not mention that Nadir is a co-founder of one of my favorite organizations,", "Climate Up Close", "-- whose work largely inspired my approach to this project. After Nadir's interview, it was 1:30 PM, and there was still another 24 miles to walk in the day. It was time to get moving. Fortunately, reinforcements were brought in to keep momentum going. Namely, one Dr. Andrew Williams. See the first few miles of Andrew and me traversing the Institute Woods pathways in the video below!", "With Andrew by my side, the miles went a bit faster. It was extremely fun to discuss clouds, and we even saw a beautiful heron swoop over the Delaware and Raritan Canal State Park Trail. After the canal trail, we emerged onto the streets of Lawrence Township, where I was thrilled to be able to hand out more WC Livestream stickers at various chance encounters with folks. One standout instance was with a sales associate at EZ-Go mart. In our conversation, we commiserated over the hot weather, and they provided some tips regarding nice roads to walk on for the next leg of the journey. The conversation, coupled with the refreshing drinks, provided a much needed boost that helped us chip off the next 20 miles a bit easier!", "1/3 of the way there!", "Andrew and I continued along past Lawrence Township, and into Trenton, where we reached the Delaware River-- and the second state crossing of the trip! To see the Delaware River, and all other water bodies I faced along the route, click below.", "🌊", "Plot waterways on the map", "Check out Andrew and me crossing the Lower Trenton Bridge into Morrisville, PA!", "The Morrisville mileswent quickly with Andrew's company, and sped up even more when we met up with Andrew’s friend, Dr. Adam Sokol-- a former GFDL scientist, and incoming Earth Science professor at the University of Cincinnati. Adam also generously brought some baked goods, which were very well received. :D", "Adam, Andrew, and myself continued along, and found ourselves in Levittown just as the Sun began to set. Here, we were delighted to run into a resident shooting hoops in their driveway. Half-jokingly, I asked if I could take a shot, and before we knew it, we were all shooting hoops and talking science. To our delight, this driveway basketball enthusiast, Steve, had done air quality research on Whiteface Mountain growing up! He was very supportive of the trip.", "It was a big boost to meet Steve, and my spirits were high heading into the final miles of this long, long day. After the hooping encounter, Adam headed back to his car, which he had parked a few miles back, and Andrew and I continued on for some of the most tiring miles of the entire trip-- Miles 25-30 of the day. Eventually, we made it to our final destination, a Holiday Inn in Bensalem, PA, where we met the rest of the Content Crew who were busy creating content. I slept very deeply, after the nearly 30 miles of walking and four interviews!", "The next morning, Andrew was obliged with", "an interview", "about the highly anticipated WC Livestream, so I started the day solo, with anticipation of Andrew re-joining mid-day for some miles. The goal destination for the day was Temple University, where I had an interview scheduled with Dr. Becki Beadling at 4:00 PM, plus or minus a few hours. The Bensalem streets I took in the morning soon gave way to Northeast Philadelphia, where I was joined by Andrew for the final few miles to Temple University. See the adventures from this section in the video below!", "Upon our arrival, I met Dr. Becki Beadling for interview #8 at Temple's Science Education and Research Center (SERC). Becki is a world renowned expert in all things ocean circulation, and a chapter author on the upcoming 7th Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change Assessment Report-- arguably the world's most important synthesis of the state of human caused climate change. Becki is also actively involved with science communication, as a leader of", "Triple C at Triple B", ", a coveted climate science public lecture series. Watch Interview #8 ft. Becki below!", "One of my favorite parts of Becki's interview is how it connects to the interviews 50 miles earlier at GFDL. Namely, she cites how important it is for climate models to be developed, such as the one at GFDL, so that cutting edge research can be conducted downstream at universities. Following the interview, Becki led Virginia, Miriam, Andrew, and me to Victory Brewing Company for happy hour with members of her Ocean Climate Connections lab.", "The happy hour was another standout moment of the walk, featuring conversation about Argo Floats, and our favorite climate and weather science fun facts. Additionally, the happy hour marked the arrival of Tufts Earth Science Professor, Dr. Jonah Bloch-Johnson-- lead organizer of the WC Livestream, acclaimed composer of music, and world class human. Jonah graciously agreed to join for Day #6 of the walk, as Andrew departed the path to begin prepping for the 50 Hour WC Livestream set to start on June 1. I love this photo of Jonah in the content bus, just having arrived from the Amtrak from Boston — looks like an album cover!", "With Jonah in town, we both prepared for the next day's marathon with a good nights sleep-- Jonah with a friend in the city, and the rest of the content crew in Glassboro, NJ, courtesy of Virginia's family home. 100 miles done!", "The sixth day of the trip was exciting for a number of reasons. Perhaps most of all, because Jonah was able to join, and he brought a ukulele!! As Jonah and I traveled from the sidewalks of Philly to Ridley Park, we had our first notable stop for lunch at a place called Alex's Shorties, which was right next to a haircutting establishment called Penny's Cuts. While Jonah and I awaited our orders from Shorties, we were thrilled to meet one of the haircutting professionals from Penny's, whom wished us luck on our travels, and was excited to receive some WC stickers!", "Re-fueled up, Jonah and I started back on our journey, but just one mile later found ourselves entering a Family Dollar in the adjacent town, Chester, to hunt for some electrolytes. While this stop was meant to be quick, it quickly evolved into a full-fledged conversation about weather and climate with a talented local artist and store employee--", "@1random9", "-- who proved to be extremely supportive of the walk!", "The conversation and cold drinks from this stop was a pivotal moment in the day, and Jonah and I set off with new vigor for the final 15 miles of the day. Many, many ukulele tunes later, Jonah and I made it to our final destination of the day, the lovely Radnor Green Park in Claymont, Delaware. This marked the 3rd state crossing of the trip! The video below recaps this wonderful section of the walk.", "After making it to Radnor Green Park, Jonah and I were picked up in the Content Bus and headed to nearby Eddystone, PA to grab dinner and re-supply on snacks at a ShipRite.", "Following the ShopRite pitstop, we headed to the rest stop for the day-- my aunt's house, in nearby Wilmington, DE! It was extremely fun to see her and my cousin, and to introduce them to the rest of the walking crew. Their hospitality was incredible, and I'm extremely grateful for them helping make this trip happen.", "The next morning, I prepared for more solo miles, with Jonah returning his focus to final organizing logistics for the 50 hour Livestream (which was three days away at this point). Before the miles began, however, Interviews #8 and #9 of the trip were set to happen at Radnor Green Park, non-coincidentally where Jonah and I had left off the previous day. The first interview was with Dr. Andra Garner, a professor at Rowan University and a world leading expert on coastal flooding risk. Watch interview #9 ft. Andra below!", "Andra's interview was impactful in many ways, one being that she described a very local application of climate research, bringing it home to the east US. It was also poignant how Andra describes how federal budget cuts now will most certainly shrink the amount of people working on weather and climate research for years to come. Following Andra’s interview was Dr. Lauren Kipp, also a professor at Rowan University, and a world leading expert in chemical oceanography. Watch Interview #10 ft. Lauren below!", "Perhaps my favorite part about Lauren's interview is how it ties in with Nadir's interview 70 miles earlier. Namely, one thing Nadir details is the famous Arctic amplification prediction made by Syukuro Manabe and Kirk Bryan in the 1970s, and here we are 50 years later, with Lauren spending much of her scientific career studying the ocean-related impacts of this exact phenomenon happening. Another important part of Lauren's interview is how she describes how the recent Office of Management and Budget (OMB) proposal may undermine scientific studies. For those unfamiliar, this OMB proposal is formally known as the \"Regulation for Federal Financial Assistance\" -- which would essentially shift federal research grant allocation decisions away from independent scientific experts, and into the hands of political appointees.", "With the Interviews #8 and #9 done, I continued my journey through Radnor Green Park, with my GPS set for the University of Delaware for the next batch of expert interviews. Along the way, I ran into a really, really cool secret tunnel...", "This tunnel was shockingly energizing to me, and fueled the next few miles before a chance encounter with some firefighters in Wilmington. Halfway there!!!!", "A few miles down the road from Radnor Green, I found myself in a chance encounter with a firefighting crew outside Fire Station #5 in Wilmington, DE. While I originally intended to just ask where the nearest restroom was, a conversation was quickly sparked when I mentioned the walk I was on! As we chatted, one firefighter expressed concern over the recent drought that had been impacting Wilmington, which had exacerbated residential fire risk. He then described how useful seasonal climate forecasts were for preparing for this regional risk.", "Though unplanned, this was one of the more memorable moments of the entire walk; it exemplified an entire local community benefiting from the weather and climate research all of these experts along the path had been describing.", "The next 12 miles of the day were a bit of a slog, but as I navigated through the town of Christiana and eventually into Newark, I approached the site of Interview #10 of the trip, with Dr. Charles Schutte (Virginia's husband!), at the University of Delaware's campus. For the final mile leading up to the interview, I was thrilled to be joined by Charles, Virginia, and their two brilliant kids, who both boosted the energy and reinvigorated my motivation. We even made some paper airplanes, and they taught me how to eat honeysuckle nectar!", "Charles is a professor and world leading expert in aquatic biogeochemistry, and has played a major role in coastal wetland restoration efforts throughout New Jersey. Watch Interview #11 ft. Charles below!", "To hear Charles’s echo the concerns from Lauren 21 miles prior regarding the potential OMB changes was powerful, specifically his point about the potential consequences for the grant-allocating process. Additionally, learning about Charles research was incredible; his work serving as a tangible example of how scientific inquiry can lead to local climate change solutions. Following the interviews Charles and the kids parted ways, while the content crew headed back to my aunt's house for another night in Delaware. At my aunt's house, we were delighted to be taken care of by Luis the dog, providing a boost for the final few days of this long, long adventure. I love this photo of Miriam and Louis!", "Five days to go!", "With just over 100 miles to the finish line, I started day eight with an interview with Dr. Kelsey Malloy, a professor at University of Delaware, and world leading expert in climate variability and extreme weather. Watch Interview #12 ft. Kelsey below!", "Notable, Kelsey details an early experience with the NOAA Earth Systems Research Lab, that in large part helped her become the scientist she is today. Hearing this testimony was extremely topical, given the point Andra had made in her interview 21 miles prior-- how losing federal funding now will likely reduce the number of future climate scientists. Secondly, I found there to be many parallels with Becki's interview, with regards to the cutting edge science Kelsey does; research in large part made possible downstream of federally funded climate models, such as the National Center for Atmospheric Research (NCAR). Through these interviews, it became more and more clear to me how important this relationship between federal funding and universities really is, and what we stand to lose if we cut funding.", "After Kelsey's interview, she was kind to treat me and the content crew to some coffee, which kept me awake for the next 13 miles, as I made my way out of Newark, and into Maryland-- the 4th state crossing of the trip! In Maryland, I navigated the Elk Mills sidewalks into North East, where I had yet another fun chance encounter with Leo, of Leo's Smash Burgers & Dogs.", "After the re-fueling from Leo's, I trudged along for the final seven miles of the day, through Charlestown, and eventually to Perryville near the shores of the Susquehanna River-- the third major river crossing I'd face on the trip. To see firsthand what the miles between the Kelsey interview and the Susquehanna shore were like, see below.", "When I reached Perryville Community Park, I had originally planned to kayak across the Susquehanna River to Havre De Grace. However, the kayak rental place had permanently closed the day before I arrived! Talk about unfortunate timing. With the kayak plans foiled, I called it a day, and was picked up by the content crew to head to a nearby Hilton hotel to get some highly anticipated shut-eye. Eight days down, four to go.", "The start of day nine began with a Content Bus assist over the Susquehanna River across the Thomas J. Hatem Bridge to Concord Point Park in Havre De Grace, to mimic the planned kayak crossing. Here, I began my steps, bracing for another solo marathon day. Late in the morning, my spirits were boosted when I saw two virtual videos had been sent in from experts who were not able to meet-up in person, but still wanted to be a part of the project! The first such video was sent in from the world renowned director of the 5th National Climate Assessment (NCA), Allison Crimmins. See bonus virtual video #1 ft. Allison below!", "Having Allison send in a video was particularly surreal, because one of my motivations for this walk was that 400 scientists/experts working on the 6th NCA had recently been dismissed (see", "). In the video, it's clear that just as the firefighters I had met in Wilmington use weather and climate data to keep their community safe, the NCA helps communities understand and adapt to their climate risks-- undoubtably bolstering how research benefits American society. The second of the two videos sent in was from Molly Robey-- a faculty meteorologist at Loyola University Maryland, and PhD student at Johns Hopkins University. Molly is also an acclaimed science communicator, who has worked extensively on the", "Baltimore Social-Environmental Collaborative", ". See bonus virtual video #2 ft. Molly below!", "What I love about Molly's video, like Allison's, is that it really brings home the perspective of doing weather and climate research for a purpose-- to help create policy and guidance for communities to adapt to dangerous changes. This crucial step of taking scientific findings -- like from Charles's wetland research 31 miles prior -- and using them to create science-informed improvements to the well-being of communities, is nothing short of inspiring.", "With a motivational boost from the videos Molly and Allison sent in, I powered along past Havre De Grace towards another very notable stop-- Aberdeen Proving Ground. As Nadir mentioned in his interview, the first numerical weather forecast was developed at the Institute for Advanced Study, but coincidentally, it was physically performed at Aberdeen Proving Ground-- the exact place I walked by to start my day #9 trek! In honor of this moment, one of the short videos the content team created during the walk was dedicated to it,", "linked here", ". To see a recap of the five mile stretch of this section that included the Aberdeen Proving Grounds pass by-- see the recap video below.", "Day #9 was also special, because with the help of Virginia and Miriam, I was able to host a Reddit \"Ask Me Anything\" -- check it out", "! (Shout-out Virginia for taking the lead on this; it was a blast!) As the final 10 miles of the day came to a close, featuring Belcamp, Abingdon, Joppatowne, and eventually Nottingham, I found myself resting up at WoodSpring Suites, where I met two incredible employees.", "200 miles down!!", "I started Day #10 with two unexpected highlights at WoodSpring Suites, the first being an exceptionally skinny truck sighting in the parking lot as I got up for an early breakfast and walked around the parking lot.", "Secondly, the two workers who checked me out proved to be extremely kind, and gave me a big boost of energy going into the final two days of the walk!", "As I departed WoodSpring Suites and headed southwest, I was feeling a bit more ambitious than usual, with 30 miles planned on the day. That ambition proved even greater than I had imagined, and I somehow found myself walking 52 miles continuously, the 2nd most I have ever walked in a day! I past through Nottingham, Rossville, Overlea, Frankford, Belair-Edison, Baltimore, Arbutus, Elkridge, Jessup, Savage, Laurel, Beltsville, and eventually into College Park, the site of the final two interviews of the walk the following day. To see some clips of this double marathon effort, see below!", "Miriam was an incredible support crew member-- staying up until 3 AM, picking me up, and bringing me back to her incredibly generous uncle’s house in Bethesda, MD-- our home base for the final few days of the trip.", "While finishing the 52 mile was a big highlight, it was not the most memorable thing that happened on the day. That belonged to the 2nd annual WC Livestream kickoff that happened at 3:00 PM, 12 hours earlier! While I was busy with the walk, Jonah, Andrew, Miriam, and Virginia were busy hosting and spearheading the livestream at Miriam's uncle's house, where weather and climate experts from across the US began to share their science in real-time. If you have enjoyed the expert interviews featured in this walk, you can find 50 more hours of video footage from various experts linked", "While the livestream kicking off was incredibly exciting, another development was underway, namely, details were becoming finalized regarding my letter handoff to members of Congress. If it's not obvious by now, I wasn't actually carrying the 300 letters I ended up delivering to Congress, but rather, members of the WC Livestream team, led by Marc Alessi and Virginia Schutte, were leading letter collection efforts virtually and preparing for them to be printed off in time for the press conference handoff. As I returned home in the Content Bus at 3:00 AM, I received word from Miriam that the press conference details had been confirmed: I was to meet seven members of Congress at the House Triangle at 2:00 PM on June 3rd, where I was to give a speech about my walk, and hand off the letters upon my arrival. Additionally, speakers from the American Geophysical Union (AGU) -- the country’s largest nonprofit organization of Earth and space scientists -- along with WC Livestream organizers and members of Congress, would give speeches.", "I couldn't quite believe all of this was transpiring, and having walked 52 miles, I half wondered if I was dreaming all of this!", "Walking 52 miles in a 13 hour stretch proved to be quite tiring, and so I slept in to 11 AM before heading back to the University of Maryland for the final two interviews of the walk. The first interview was with Dr. Ross Salawitch, a professor of Earth Science at University of Maryland, and a world renowned expert in quantifying the effects of human activity on climate. Watch Interview #13 ft. Ross below!", "Ross's interview was incredible for many reasons, but one big takeaway I had was how scientific inquiry regarding atmospheric aerosols has led to much cleaner air for the citizens of Maryland to breathe. I thought this was an amazing perspective to have, complementing the sentiment shared from both the firefighters 115 miles prior, and Allison's NCA video: weather and climate research benefits all Americans. It was also jarring to hear Ross describe having no knowledge of when future funding opportunities will emerge, serving as a reminder of the purpose of this walk, and the urgency of the situation.", "On a non-science related note, I also would be remiss without noting that Ross was an incredible host,", "showing me around the University of Maryland campus", ", and buying me ice cream. He was extremely kind to take time out of his day for this, to which I am extremely grateful! The second interview at University of Maryland, and the final (!) interview of the walk, was with Dr. Rob Levy, a world leading expert on atmospheric aerosols. Watch Interview #14 ft. Rob below!", "To end the walk with Rob's interview was nothing less than surreal for me, because my PhD project is in large part possible because of the work Rob has done with MODIS (the Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer, an instrument aboard NASA’s Terra satellite). In honor of this, the base-map of the map to the right is an image from MODIS, taken at the exact moment this interview happened (well, technically it was 2 hours later. :). Following the interviews with Ross and Rob, I set out for a much less ambitious nine mile day, where I navigated from College park, through Chillum, and into Washington, D.C.-- just a few miles outside the Capitol building. This marked the 5th and final border crossing of the trip. Check out this section here!", "After making it to Fairmont Street, myself and the content crew headed back to Miriam's uncle's house, where the 50 hour Livestream continued into it's second day. It was a real treat to watch some talks, but I quickly found myself needing sleep, so I headed to bed extra early, and prepared for the final day of the walk.", "Tomorrow was going to be epic!!", "June 3, 2026", "The final day", "Day #12 began with a couple miles with Miriam and Virginia, followed by a delicious smoothie from the Eat Well Juice Bar-- essential fuel for the crazy day to come! Soon, Google Maps indicated the Capitol Building was just 3.4 miles away.", "Before we made it to the Capitol building, there was one more stop on my list, and it happened to be a major highlight-- the", "Museum of Unnatural Disasters", ". The organizers of this popup museum in DC had invited me to stop by on my way to the Capitol building, to check out spectacular exhibits bringing awareness to the increased natural disaster risk with a warming earth’s surface temperatures. These exhibits reminded me of so many moments from the walk-- two such being Andra 137 miles prior describing how increased ocean temperatures are supercharging hurricanes, and Kelsey 116 miles prior describing the interplay between the changing climate and increased frequency of extreme temperatures. This museum was an incredible public display of the very same science Andra and Kelsey have dedicated their careers to helping uncover. After a quick photoshoot at the exhibit (", "scroll halfway down the article!", "), I headed for a final sprint to make it to the press conference in time! Spoiler-- I made it!!!!!!", "WALK COMPLETE!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!! 🎉🎉🎉🎉", "It was surreal to arrive at the Capitol building, and even more so to see cameras set up, ready to capture the press conference event.", "At the press conference, I was thrilled to give a speech, and did my best to summarize what had been truly an incredible adventure. See the speech below. :)", "After my speech, I handed the nearly 300 letters (many of which were virtually sent in, and kindly printed out by Marc Alessi and Jonah beforehand ;) directly into the hands of the members of Congress. It was one of the most surreal moments of my life, and I was filled with gratitude... and exhaustion. After conveying my extreme gratitude to the folks who showed up for me, I returned to Miriam’s uncle’s house, where I refueled, and where the 50 hour livestream of science continued on strong for it's final few hours.", "The Livestream was a smashing success, featuring countless brilliant speakers, and brilliant hosts. While I didn't have too much time during the walk to view it, I have since loved watching through the weather and climate science talks, and I highly recommend the stream to anyone who missed it (", "Link here", "). At 5:00 PM, June 3rd, the Livestream ended, with a recap video of my walk, incredibly put together by Miriam in just one short afternoon (", "see here", "). With that, the Livestream, and the NYC to DC walk, had concluded.", "There is far too much to unpack with words here, but if you feel inclined, I've written out some reflections in the following section, as this story reaches it's close. :)", "THE END", "Reflection", "As I am writing this out right now, three months later, from my first steps, I truly can’t believe this walk happened, and I am unbelievably proud and grateful to be able to put it into a final form here on this website. It goes without saying that a walk like this takes immense privilege, and I couldn’t have done it without the endless support of my fiancée, my family and the friends who have all made many sacrifices for this to happen. To them, I extend sincere gratitude, and I will end this piece with a brief reflection selfishly for my closure, and also to thank them.", "To begin, I will continue talking a bit about what happened after the Livestream ended. After the livestream ended, all co-organizers hopped on a Zoom call to reflect on the event. See image below. It was a collective sigh of relief, and a moment to celebrate. :)", "After the call, some of the group, including myself, headed to the National Mall for a walk to take a moment to be present with everything that just happened. This was extremely emotional for many folks including myself, as we looked at great American landmarks, and thought about what this country has been through in its multi-hundred-year history.", "As we sat and reflected, I thought of all the things that had happened on this walk. For one, I turned 24! Definitely one of my most memorable birthdays to date.", "Also, I walked 270.4 miles! That's a long way, and a big personal accomplishment for me, after spending a few years battling some pretty debilitating joint pain. This was one of the first times in a few years I've been able to tackle a big physical effort like this without pain, and for that, I am extremely grateful. As a final reflection on this physical effort, I've plotted the elevation profile of the route below.", "×", "3 mile rolling median elevation from my Garmin Forerunner 95 data. :)", "Elevation profile of the walk... see this over the whole map by clicking below!", "⛰", "Plot topography on the map", "That moment to reflect at the National Mall was an extremely meaningful first step in processing the walk, and I have since continued processing the walk over a very busy summer, somehow finding the time to put this entire project together. For those curious, editing these videos and programming this site took around 300 hours, which is not a small amount of time. :) This time does not include the walk itself, which was around 90 hours of walking time alone, not counting stops, interviews, video editing, and stayovers. Considering the initial planning stages of this (the month leading up), the walk itself, and the editing of this website in its entirety, I estimate this was around 600 hours of work in total, which is almost certainly the hardest I’ve ever worked on a passion project. If you are still reading to this point, you are too kind for letting me take up your time, and I thank you immensely for spending time on this site. :)", "I want to wrap up this site just by reflecting on a few other NYC to DC walks that helped me frame/ develop mine. One was documented in a book called", "American Ramble", "by Neil King, and I read it in its entirety after finishing my walk, and I highly recommend it for anyone interested in learning more about the historical sites and histories of the land between these two cities. Another was documented in a", "Youtube video", "by Step-n-Out. They put together an incredible documentary that I watched multiple times as inspiration. Both Step-n-Out and Neil King did the walk in part because they were facing illness (in Neil’s case, terminal), and that has really resonated with me. I was not facing a personal illness, but in many ways, the scientific community is. I think there’s something healing about going somewhere by foot. And there’s no doubt in my mind about that.", "Lastly, I hope it’s obvious with this project that funding weather and climate science is an economic investment that has massive returns-- not a cost to society. Knowing how to prepare for disasters is universally a good thing for society, and I hope that is made clear with this piece.", "I wrap up this reflection with a reiteration of my sincere gratitude to each and every person who partook in the project, supported me through the whole process, and/ or has taken a stand to protect the national treasure that is the weather and climate research infrastructure here in the United States. Additionally, if you have read this far, you are truly too kind for letting me take up your time, and I extend my sincere thanks to you as well.", "Until the next adventure,", "--Syl, signing off for now.", "It turns out most of the paparazzi shots from my walk came from space. (lol!) This one's from MODIS — a refrigerator-sized camera aboard NASA's Terra satellite, 438 miles up and moving at 16,800 mph. MODIS captured this on June 2nd, my second-to-last day. To learn more about the mission, see the interview with Rob Levy at the University of Maryland stop. :)", "Overlays", "Hover only", "Water", "Borders", "Off", "USA", "CA", "Rainbow", "Colour", "Opacity", "Weather", "Radar", "Temp", "Dew", "▶ Loop", "Bonus layers", "Bedrock age", "Topography", "Overlay opacity", "Image from MODIS aboard NASA's Terra satellite (6/2/26)", "N", "walked", "MASSACHUSETTS", "CONNECTICUT", "NEW YORK", "PENNSYLVANIA", "NEW JERSEY", "WEST VIRGINIA", "DELAWARE", "MARYLAND", "VIRGINIA", "Source: MODIS onboard Terra", "Call your reps", "Cursor over the map to highlight borders/water features!", "Image from MODIS aboard NASA's Terra satellite, taken on June 2nd. I'm somewhere near the star marking DC, lol!", "More walk content:", "Videos", "Livestream", "Blogs", "Comments", "George", "September 19, 2026 · 9:12 AM", "Wonderful work, I'm very inspired. This site is remarkable.", "Name", "Email", "optional", "Post anonymously", "Comment", "Leave this field empty", "Post comment", "Share full article", "References", "Next", "Last updated September 19, 2026", "Enter full screen", "Exit full screen", "How a message in an organizing chat became 270 miles of sidewalk — the plan, the merch and the crew.", "Mile zero. The first steps, out of the park and down the length of Manhattan.", "The southern tip of Manhattan, and the last of the island before the water.", "The far southern tip of Staten Island, before the bridge into New Jersey.", "A green stretch of Middlesex County.", "A Legion hall in South Brunswick.", "Princeton and NOAA’s Geophysical Fluid Dynamics Laboratory (GFDL), with Nadir Jeevanjee, Mitch Bushuk, Tim Merlis, and Bernadette Woods Placky.", "The woods behind Princeton.", "Crossing the Delaware on foot, out of New Jersey.", "The end of a long day in Bensalem, PA.", "North Philadelphia, with Becki Beadling.", "Claymont, DE, with Andra Garner and Lauren Kipp.", "A firehouse in Wilmington.", "Newark, DE, with Kelsey Malloy.", "Past the Proving Ground, into Perryville.", "A night in Nottingham, MD.", "College Park, with Ross Salawitch and Rob Levy.", "The Capitol steps, nearly 300 letters, and seven members of Congress.", "What 270 miles of sidewalk did and did not change — and what came after the letters were handed over.", "Read section", "Show less", "Read", "Unread", "Interviews:"], "cues": {"allegra": [[0.166, 3.837, "Hey, I'm Syl and I'm walking 250 miles from New York City to D.C."], [3.878, 6.006, "in support of weather and climate science."], [6.006, 9.009, "At mile nine, I spoke with Dr. Allegra LeGrande."], [9.009, 14.222, "Hi, my name is Dr. Allegra LeGrande, and I'm a climate scientist, a mom"], [14.222, 15.591, "and a Manhattanite."], [15.591, 18.977, "How did you get involved with climate research?"], [19.06, 23.231, "I got into climate research because I got a degree in geophysics"], [23.273, 24.607, "and a degree in anthropology."], [24.607, 29.112, "And the area where those two things intersect is in past climates,"], [29.112, 31.906, "and how past climates might have changed"], [31.906, 33.575, "the way societies themselves could develop."], [33.575, 36.244, "How do we know what the climate was like in the past?"], [36.244, 37.759, "One of the best tools we have"], [37.759, 40.79, "comes from these wonderful archives called water isotopes."], [40.79, 43.418, "It turns out that if you have a couple of extra neutrons,"], [43.418, 46.463, "either stuck onto the hydrogen bits or stuck onto the oxygen bit,"], [46.504, 49.466, "that whole molecule moves around the climate system"], [49.466, 51.819, "in a very different way, and in a way"], [51.819, 54.554, "that is consistently influenced by climate."], [54.554, 56.878, "You can look in ocean sediment cores,"], [56.878, 60.018, "at the tiny calcareous shells of marine organisms."], [60.018, 63.521, "And based on the amount of the heavy isotope"], [63.563, 66.399, "incorporated into their bodies, you can tell what the temperature was"], [66.399, 67.901, "when those bodies were forming."], [67.901, 71.404, "How do we know that our toolkit for looking at the climate of the future"], [71.404, 72.197, "is any good?"], [72.197, 74.491, "We vet that toolkit by testing it out"], [74.491, 75.158, "in the past."]], "alan": [[0.083, 1.042, "Hey, I'm Syl"], [1.042, 4.963, "and I'm walking 250 miles in support of weather and climate research,"], [4.963, 6.297, "from New York City to DC."], [6.297, 9.259, "At mile 42, I spoke with Alan Robock."], [9.259, 10.301, "My name is Alan Robock."], [10.301, 12.22, "I'm a professor at Rutgers University."], [12.22, 15.932, "How do you view the responsibility of scientists to speak out"], [15.932, 18.685, "if they find something that society could benefit from knowing?"], [18.685, 21.229, "I've taken millions of dollars in tax money"], [21.229, 24.607, "over my career to do research, and if I find a danger to society,"], [24.607, 26.991, "I think it's my duty to warn people about it."], [26.991, 28.528, "The most important example is"], [28.528, 30.363, "what would happen if there was a nuclear war."], [30.363, 33.408, "There would be smoke from fires that would go up in the atmosphere"], [33.7, 36.661, "and block the sun and make it cold and dark at the Earth's surface."], [36.661, 39.998, "You could have a nuclear winter, and that would sentence most of the people"], [39.998, 43.793, "in the world to death by famine, because you couldn't grow any crops."], [43.793, 47.005, "I had a student from Cuba, and he sent me an email once and said,"], [47.005, 48.59, "can you come to Cuba and talk about this?"], [48.59, 50.759, "And it turned out Fidel Castro was interested."], [50.759, 54.721, "So I gave a talk with Fidel Castro sitting there in the audience."], [54.721, 58.099, "On the front page of the national newspaper there, Granma, there was a whole story about it."], [58.099, 62.353, "And the next week, on his blog, Fidel Castro wrote, we have to get rid of nuclear weapons,"], [62.353, 63.592, "repeating what I said."], [63.592, 66.691, "We still have enough weapons to produce nuclear winter,"], [66.691, 67.567, "so I'm still concerned about it."], [67.567, 70.653, "How might you speak about climate change to someone who had never heard of it before?"], [70.653, 73.656, "I have a ten-word elevator speech about global warming."], [73.656, 76.409, "It's real. It's us. It's bad."], [76.409, 77.812, "We're sure. There's hope."], [77.812, 81.122, "Humans are burning fossil fuels, coal, oil and natural gas,"], [81.122, 83.208, "and putting carbon dioxide into the atmosphere,"], [83.208, 85.502, "and that traps the heat and makes the Earth warmer."], [85.502, 86.211, "We're sure of that,"], [86.211, 89.255, "because you can't explain the temperature change of the last 100 years"], [89.38, 91.299, "if you just looked at volcanic eruptions,"], [91.299, 94.177, "which temporarily cool the climate, and very small solar"], [94.177, 97.263, "variations. We have climate models that calculate this,"], [97.263, 99.224, "and so that's why we're sure."], [99.224, 103.144, "Could you talk more about the role that volcanoes play in climate?"], [103.561, 106.94, "A big explosive volcanic eruption can pump this gas called sulfur"], [106.94, 110.068, "dioxide up into the stratosphere, which is a layer above where we live."], [110.11, 114.489, "There's no rain to wash it out, and it forms a layer of sulfuric acid droplets,"], [114.531, 118.326, "a thin cloud, and that reflects sunlight and makes it cold at the Earth's surface."], [118.326, 122.122, "We know that because the last big eruption, in 1991, of Mount Pinatubo"], [122.122, 125.834, "in the Philippines, caused global cooling of about a degree Fahrenheit the next year."], [126.209, 129.295, "Because of this, some people have suggested, let's do it on purpose."], [129.295, 132.924, "Let's create a cloud up in the stratosphere to reduce global warming."], [132.924, 135.343, "It's called geoengineering, or climate intervention."], [135.343, 138.346, "After the Pinatubo eruption, there was a drought in India and China."], [138.388, 142.35, "So even if we kept the planet from warming, there are effects that could happen"], [142.35, 144.31, "in different places that people would not be happy with."]], "andra": [[0, 0.9, "Hey, I'm Syl"], [0.9, 3.97, "and I'm walking 250 miles from New York City to D.C."], [4.004, 6.072, "in support of weather and climate research."], [6.072, 9.209, "At mile 131, I spoke with Andra Garner."], [9.209, 10.31, "I'm Andra Garner."], [10.31, 11.811, "I'm an associate professor"], [11.811, 15.048, "at Rowan University, in the Department of Environmental Science."], [15.281, 19.586, "Are there any places in the northeast with notable recent coastal changes?"], [19.819, 24.29, "Where I'm at in New Jersey, for example, we actually have our coastline"], [24.29, 27.66, "sinking slightly as the land readjusts from the last ice age."], [27.761, 32.065, "So our sea levels are rising even faster than in other places."], [32.065, 33.867, "How about coastal storms?"], [33.867, 36.77, "Is recent climate change making them worse?"], [36.77, 40.673, "One way that storms are changing is that they can tend to strengthen"], [40.673, 44.177, "more quickly than they used to on a cooler planet. One reason for that"], [44.177, 48.581, "is that these storms, like hurricanes, feed off warm ocean waters,"], [48.581, 52.018, "and the vast majority of the warming that humans have caused"], [52.085, 53.586, "has gone into our oceans."], [53.586, 56.589, "So we're really kind of supercharging that environment."], [56.623, 59.392, "How has federal funding played a role in your research,"], [59.392, 61.861, "and in your field's work more generally?"], [61.861, 65.198, "A lot of my work is only possible because of the federal funding"], [65.198, 66.332, "that I have."], [66.332, 70.17, "When we lose that funding, everyone is more at risk."], [70.17, 73.64, "We know that right now there are places that are becoming uninsurable,"], [73.64, 76.776, "whether because of wildfires or because of flooding."], [76.976, 80.38, "We need to have the science to understand how those hazards are changing."], [80.647, 85.118, "And the last thing I'll say is that when we cut scientific funding,"], [85.218, 87.12, "we're cutting off those pipelines"], [87.12, 91.391, "that help to train our future scientists, the ones going to improve our forecasts."]], "becki": [[0.125, 0.667, "Hello."], [0.667, 4.379, "I'm Syl and I'm walking 250 miles from New York City to D.C."], [4.421, 6.631, "in support of weather and climate research."], [6.631, 10.26, "At mile 106, I spoke with Becki Beadling."], [10.301, 11.761, "My name is Becki Beadling"], [11.761, 15.64, "and I'm an assistant professor at Temple University here in Philadelphia."], [15.64, 19.269, "What is AMOC, and how does ocean circulation happen?"], [19.394, 23.44, "AMOC is the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation, and it's part"], [23.44, 26.693, "of the global overturning circulation, specifically within the Atlantic."], [26.735, 30.697, "This overturning circulation is responsible for transporting heat"], [30.697, 31.99, "and salt from the tropics"], [31.99, 35.827, "to the high northern latitudes, where those waters lose"], [35.827, 39.831, "that heat to the overlying atmosphere, and they actually sink and overturn."], [39.831, 42.656, "What should we know about the Antarctic ice sheet?"], [42.656, 44.294, "The Antarctic ice sheet holds"], [44.335, 45.879, "a ton of fresh water,"], [45.879, 49.674, "the equivalent of about 58 metres of global sea level rise."], [49.758, 52.719, "We are looking at a future right now where we are going to see"], [52.719, 55.889, "a significant fraction of that meltwater"], [55.889, 59.768, "from the ice sheet entering our oceans and contributing to global sea level rise."], [59.768, 63.563, "But when you add fresh water on top of a salty ocean,"], [63.605, 68.318, "you cause large changes in ocean circulation, and you can also affect"], [68.359, 69.569, "atmospheric circulation"], [69.569, 73.406, "and disrupt global precipitation patterns, for instance."], [73.656, 77.16, "How does the ocean influence the warming we feel in the atmosphere?"], [77.16, 80.955, "I always like to say that global warming really is ocean warming,"], [80.955, 84.584, "because if we look at where the excess heat is going on"], [84.584, 87.587, "our planet, over 90% of it is going into the ocean."], [87.712, 90.048, "Water has a really high heat capacity,"], [90.048, 93.802, "so it can take up a lot of heat for a small change in temperature,"], [93.843, 96.805, "unlike the atmosphere, which has a low heat capacity."], [96.805, 99.39, "So you can change the temperature of the atmosphere really rapidly."], [99.39, 103.311, "But the ocean has the ability to just take up heat, and on top of that,"], [103.353, 106.564, "it has circulation that can sequester"], [106.564, 109.067, "that heat at depth, away from the surface."], [109.067, 111.528, "So you can have heat stored within the ocean."], [111.528, 114.614, "One reason we care about it, particularly in the Southern Ocean,"], [114.614, 118.201, "is because that ocean is right next to a giant ice sheet."], [118.243, 121.329, "How has federal funding made all the work you do possible?"], [121.704, 126.251, "We rely on these federal organisations, particularly NCAR,"], [126.376, 129.546, "GFDL and GISS, to do a lot of the model development,"], [129.546, 133.049, "which then produces the climate models that we can use for science."], [133.049, 137.679, "But a lot of the science comes from research institutions, and making sure"], [137.679, 142.142, "those simulations keep being developed is critical to pushing the science forward."], [142.142, 146.521, "A lot of that work is done at universities, but it relies on access to"], [146.563, 148.064, "those federal resources."]], "bernadette": [[0.041, 0.917, "Hey, I'm Syl"], [0.917, 4.212, "and I'm walking 250 miles from New York City to D.C."], [4.254, 6.339, "in support of weather and climate research."], [6.339, 9.064, "At mile 56, I spoke with Bernadette Woods Placky."], [9.064, 11.011, "My name is Bernadette Woods Placky."], [11.469, 14.945, "I have been a meteorologist for more than 25 years."], [14.945, 17.058, "How are weather forecasts made?"], [17.058, 21.104, "There are a lot of ingredients that go into making a forecast."], [21.104, 24.482, "First, we need observations of what's going on in the world."], [24.524, 25.9, "What is the temperature?"], [25.9, 26.86, "What is the wind?"], [26.86, 28.778, "How much moisture do we have in the atmosphere?"], [28.778, 30.655, "When you take all of that data,"], [30.655, 33.7, "you put it together and you put it into computer models."], [33.7, 36.108, "You take all of that information, place it together,"], [36.108, 38.655, "and you can come out with a forecast on the other side."], [38.655, 40.415, "Is there a best weather app out there?"], [40.415, 44.419, "One that we almost all have on our phone and use the most is RadarScope."], [44.711, 47.255, "You've got the layers of what's producing lightning or not,"], [47.255, 50.925, "and you can get the rain versus snow element of it too."], [50.967, 54.929, "So RadarScope is my favourite weather app, and one you're going to hear a lot about"], [54.929, 56.347, "from the weather community."], [56.347, 59.893, "How can we read weather forecasting data like a pro?"], [60.143, 62.979, "My favourite weather variable is dew point."], [62.979, 66.608, "It is the most important variable for forecasting,"], [66.608, 67.442, "and I'll tell you why."], [67.442, 70.695, "Dew point means the amount of moisture in the atmosphere."], [70.695, 74.282, "When people are talking about it being really hot and muggy outside,"], [74.282, 77.035, "they're usually referencing a high dew point."], [77.035, 79.788, "So if that dew point starts to hit the 60s,"], [79.788, 81.873, "the air feels a little bit thicker out there."], [81.873, 86.795, "If it's above 65, that's an element you can look at for severe weather potential,"], [86.795, 88.213, "if there are thunderstorms coming your way."], [88.213, 93.927, "Now, dew point is also important on the other side, when you're looking at winter forecasting."], [94.052, 97.555, "You need that dew point below 32 for that precipitation"], [97.555, 100.725, "to fall as something frozen. So dew point on the cold side is"], [100.725, 104.395, "really critical for winter forecasting, and on the warm side really"], [104.395, 108.233, "critical for severe weather forecasting, and for how you feel outside."], [108.233, 111.236, "And so when people say it's humid, actually 100%"], [111.236, 114.239, "humidity happens when it's snowing at 32 degrees."], [114.28, 117.826, "What you really mean is that the dew point is likely up in the 60s to 70."], [117.826, 119.119, "And that's something you want to pay attention to."]], "charles": [[0.083, 1.543, "Hey, I'm Syl and I'm walking"], [1.543, 5.171, "250 miles in support of weather and climate research."], [5.255, 8.258, "At mile 152, I spoke with Charles Schutte."], [8.341, 9.342, "Hi, I'm Charles Schutte."], [9.342, 10.969, "I'm an associate professor in the Department"], [10.969, 12.846, "of Environmental Science at Rowan University."], [12.846, 14.389, "What research do you do?"], [14.389, 17.809, "And does it have implications for addressing climate change?"], [17.809, 19.477, "Most of my research right now is in wetlands."], [19.477, 21.688, "When the soil is wet, there's no oxygen in the soil,"], [21.688, 23.732, "and that means it's a place where carbon can get buried,"], [23.732, 25.984, "so they can have a cooling effect on the climate."], [25.984, 29.779, "So if we can manage our wetlands, or add wetlands back to the landscape,"], [29.863, 32.574, "then that can have a cooling effect on the climate."], [32.574, 34.975, "How do scientists get federal funding?"], [34.975, 36.745, "As a scientist, I both write"], [36.786, 40.331, "grant applications, but I'm also on the other side of the table."], [40.331, 41.833, "I review grant applications."], [41.833, 46.296, "And so it's scientists evaluating scientists, so that we are"], [46.546, 51.843, "spending tax dollars wisely, to fund only the very best science that's likely"], [51.843, 53.919, "to have the very largest impact."], [53.919, 56.514, "What's your take on the recent OMB news?"], [56.639, 59.642, "We're hearing there will be political appointees"], [59.851, 63.646, "who are going to make these decisions about how science funding gets spent, and"], [63.855, 67.525, "not scientists. That's very concerning to me, because someone who is a political"], [67.525, 71.196, "appointee doesn't have the expertise to be making that call."], [71.279, 75.241, "Scientists are people who happen to be curious about"], [75.241, 79.37, "how the world works, and who have the training to go and find out."]], "hikaru": [[0, 4.212, "Hi, I'm Syl and I'm walking 250 miles from New York City down to D.C."], [4.337, 6.506, "in support of weather and climate science."], [6.506, 10.343, "At mile eight, I spoke with Hikaru Wakeel Hayakawa."], [10.343, 15.056, "My name is Hikaru Wakeel Hayakawa, and I am the executive director of Climate Cardinals."], [15.056, 16.933, "We are a global, youth-led organisation"], [16.933, 19.144, "breaking the language barrier in climate science."], [19.144, 21.938, "Is there a language gap in climate science?"], [21.938, 25.567, "98% of climate science is only in English."], [25.567, 28.194, "But 6.5 billion people don't speak English at all,"], [28.194, 32.407, "and there are only 400 million native English speakers in the world."], [32.407, 34.409, "When scientists are doing their work,"], [34.409, 38.455, "it's important to reach out to people from non-English-speaking institutions,"], [38.455, 42.25, "to make sure that you're capturing nuances."], [42.542, 46.629, "For example, in Arabic, the word for tornado and hurricane is the same,"], [46.629, 48.025, "so there's been an issue with emergency alerts:"], [48.025, 50.341, "when governments translate one or the other, people don't know how to respond."], [50.425, 53.136, "Is this a broader issue in the United States?"], [53.136, 56.556, "There are 50 million Americans who speak a language other than English at home,"], [56.556, 58.183, "and there have been cases all around the country"], [58.183, 60.351, "where people have not evacuated because they didn't understand"], [60.351, 61.519, "what people were telling them."], [61.519, 63.772, "Non-English-speaking communities are at the front lines"], [63.772, 65.648, "of all these environmental issues,"], [65.648, 69.069, "and a lot of them aren't able to speak to policymakers,"], [69.069, 72.28, "go to conferences, or even share what is going on"], [72.28, 72.798, "on the ground."], [72.798, 74.24, "Public funds have been at the forefront"], [74.24, 77.452, "of climate science for decades, and we need to keep delivering,"], [77.452, 80.413, "so that we can ensure communities are protected."]], "kelsey": [[0, 4.462, "Hey, I'm Syl and I'm walking 250 miles from New York City to DC"], [4.462, 6.172, "in support of weather and climate science."], [6.172, 8.768, "At mile 152 I met with Kelsey Malloy."], [8.768, 10.593, "Hi, I'm Dr. Kelsey Malloy."], [10.593, 13.638, "I'm an assistant professor at the University of Delaware,"], [13.638, 16.725, "within the Department of Geography and Spatial Sciences."], [16.725, 19.102, "What's the difference between weather and climate?"], [19.102, 24.816, "We can think of weather as a particular workout you're doing, and climate as your fitness."], [24.941, 28.278, "The amount of workouts and the types of workouts make up your fitness,"], [28.278, 30.447, "so weather can make up climate."], [30.447, 35.618, "But the other way around, your fitness determines the types of workouts you do."], [35.618, 38.747, "So let's bring this to the context of how"], [38.747, 41.916, "climate influences extreme weather events."], [41.916, 45.211, "In a warmer atmosphere, we just have more moisture,"], [45.211, 48.631, "and so if there is a precipitation event, then we can expect"], [48.631, 49.783, "more precipitation."], [49.783, 52.51, "How has federal funding impacted your career?"], [52.51, 57.307, "One of my first experiences as an undergrad researcher was at a NOAA"], [57.348, 60.06, "lab in Boulder called the Earth Systems Research Lab,"], [60.06, 62.437, "and I was part of the Physical Sciences Division,"], [62.437, 64.522, "funded by the NOAA Hollings Scholarship."], [64.522, 68.651, "And this was a really important project in understanding how climate influences"], [68.651, 69.903, "atmospheric rivers."], [69.903, 74.783, "And even throughout my entire PhD career, I used a lot of climate models"], [74.783, 77.952, "from NCAR as well, the National Center for Atmospheric Research."], [77.952, 80.997, "Are NCAR resources available to anyone?"], [81.122, 85.168, "The NCAR supercomputer is available not just to NCAR"], [85.168, 89.339, "scientists or the government, but it's a resource that's open to"], [89.38, 94.219, "all institutions across the US, and is used to better understand"], [94.219, 98.515, "weather and climate for all Americans, and actually all across the globe."], [98.598, 102.31, "At the University of Delaware, many of the faculty and students"], [102.31, 106.773, "use NCAR resources for both research and educational purposes."], [106.856, 111.236, "And this is just one example, at one institution. Imagine how"], [111.236, 115.74, "all the different educational institutions and beyond are using these NCAR resources."]], "lauren": [[0, 5.105, "Hey, I'm Syl and I'm walking 250 miles in support of weather and climate research."], [5.138, 8.259, "At mile 131, I spoke with Dr. Lauren Kipp."], [8.259, 10.043, "Hi, I'm Dr. Lauren Kipp."], [10.043, 11.244, "I'm an assistant professor"], [11.244, 14.447, "in the Department of Environmental Science at Rowan University."], [14.447, 17.017, "What are some of the smallest life forms in the ocean?"], [17.017, 18.651, "In every ocean, in every body of water,"], [18.651, 20.387, "there's microscopic life."], [20.387, 23.289, "So there are phytoplankton, which are tiny, tiny,"], [23.289, 26.426, "plant-like creatures, and zooplankton, which are tiny, tiny animal-"], [26.426, 27.227, "like creatures."], [27.227, 29.529, "And the zooplankton eat the phytoplankton,"], [29.529, 32.828, "and then bigger things eat the zooplankton, and bigger fish eat those fish."], [32.828, 33.4, "Understanding"], [33.4, 34.734, "the chemical changes that are happening"], [34.734, 38.238, "has the potential to inform our understanding of how those changes propagate up."], [38.538, 40.882, "Where in the Arctic specifically do you study?"], [40.882, 42.308, "The region that I'm studying"], [42.308, 46.98, "is the shelves north of Russia, the East Siberian Sea and the Laptev Sea."], [47.08, 49.616, "That's an important region because it's experiencing"], [49.616, 52.318, "some of the most drastic climate-driven changes."], [52.318, 56.222, "But it's also the source of an important current called the Transpolar Drift,"], [56.222, 58.037, "that carries water across the Arctic."], [58.037, 58.625, "So one thing"], [58.625, 62.429, "that we're trying to understand is how chemical changes get propagated,"], [62.429, 65.598, "not only across the Arctic Ocean, but perhaps to the North Atlantic as well."], [65.965, 70.303, "How do you view the peer review process in light of the recent OMB proposal?"], [70.37, 73.94, "I think that introducing political oversight into scientific studies would"], [74.574, 77.644, "severely undermine the objectivity and the reliability"], [77.644, 81.281, "and the trustworthiness of the results of scientific studies."]], "mitch": [[0.041, 3.878, "Hey, I'm Syl and I'm walking 250 miles in support of weather"], [3.878, 4.838, "and climate research."], [4.838, 7.799, "At mile 56, I spoke with Mitch Bushuk."], [7.799, 10.176, "My name is Mitch Bushuk, and I'm a research oceanographer"], [10.176, 14.18, "at the NOAA Geophysical Fluid Dynamics Laboratory here in Princeton, New Jersey."], [14.18, 15.974, "What is a climate model?"], [15.974, 18.184, "A climate model simulates the four components"], [18.184, 21.938, "of the climate system, which are the ocean, atmosphere, sea ice"], [21.938, 22.405, "and land."], [22.405, 26.401, "Each of those components has equations from physics that are used to describe"], [26.401, 29.946, "the laws of motion: for example, how wind flows in the atmosphere,"], [29.946, 32.907, "or how ocean currents move around in the ocean."], [32.907, 34.2, "Then the key part of the climate model"], [34.2, 36.244, "is that we couple all of those components together."], [36.244, 40.29, "The ocean will feel the winds blowing above it; the sea ice will feel warmth"], [40.29, 42.5, "coming from the ocean, which might melt it or freeze it."], [42.5, 44.377, "So all of these components are interacting."], [44.377, 48.298, "Have tools developed for climate modelling at GFDL been applied elsewhere?"], [48.465, 51.384, "The US weather forecast model, the one your phone's forecast"], [51.384, 55.346, "comes from every day: the underpinning physical model of that comes from GFDL."], [55.472, 56.639, "A model is never done."], [56.639, 59.142, "It's a continuous evolution that is always getting better."], [59.142, 62.062, "In your opinion, what's one of the most important things"], [62.062, 62.666, "GFDL does?"], [62.666, 66.775, "One key thing we do at GFDL is make seasonal to decadal predictions."], [66.775, 69.486, "And those involve developing a really high quality"], [69.486, 72.489, "set of initial conditions, or starting conditions, for the forecast."], [72.489, 74.115, "We have recently been forecasting"], [74.115, 76.618, "a very large El Nino event for this upcoming winter."], [76.618, 80.58, "Have all these forecasts been impacted by recent federal funding cuts?"], [80.789, 82.874, "We've recently lost some of our most critical staff,"], [82.874, 86.044, "the ones who developed those starting conditions for these seasonal forecasts."], [86.044, 89.172, "As we develop new models going forward that have higher fidelity,"], [89.214, 91.049, "we will no longer be able to produce the appropriate"], [91.049, 92.425, "starting conditions for these forecasts."], [92.425, 96.596, "Historically, we had 200 employees at GFDL developing these models, and recently"], [96.596, 100.058, "we've lost about 20% of our staff, so we're down to about 160 employees"], [100.058, 100.85, "within our building."], [100.85, 103.978, "We've lost really important expertise in a number of different areas,"], [103.978, 105.73, "which means that we're spread thin and we can't do"], [105.73, 107.524, "the same quality of science that we used to be able to do."]], "nadir": [[0, 0.75, "Hey, I'm Syl"], [0.75, 3.753, "and I'm walking 250 miles from New York City to D.C."], [3.795, 5.964, "in support of weather and climate science."], [5.964, 9.092, "At mile 61, I spoke with Nadir Jeevanjee."], [9.092, 10.51, "So we're standing here"], [10.51, 13.096, "at the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton, New Jersey,"], [13.096, 16.683, "which is actually the birthplace of weather and climate forecasting."], [16.683, 18.977, "How did forecasting come to be?"], [18.977, 21.98, "So behind me is the main building of the institute, Fuld Hall."], [22.897, 26.192, "In the basement there, in the late 1940s and early 1950s,"], [26.234, 28.528, "John von Neumann and his collaborators worked."], [28.528, 31.823, "Von Neumann was a Hungarian mathematician and polymath who came over from Europe."], [31.823, 34.826, "He and a team of colleagues constructed an early digital computer,"], [34.951, 38.621, "and took a simplified form of the equations that govern atmospheric motion"], [38.621, 40.707, "and put them on the computer to solve."], [40.707, 44.627, "In doing that, they generated the first valid weather forecast."], [44.627, 46.212, "The calculation itself, on this early computer,"], [46.212, 48.757, "took about 24 hours to make a 24-hour forecast."], [48.757, 51.468, "So not that valuable initially, but with practice"], [51.468, 54.846, "they sped it up, and they handed the technology off to the National Weather Service."], [54.846, 58.892, "Those computerized forecasts became the digital forecasts that get"], [58.933, 61.227, "beamed into our pockets, and that we look at every day."], [61.227, 65.023, "Are these early weather models connected in any way to the climate models"], [65.023, 65.623, "we have today?"], [65.623, 68.151, "In addition to being the birthplace of weather forecasting,"], [68.151, 70.403, "von Neumann realized that these weather models,"], [70.403, 74.199, "that simulate the flow of the atmosphere, could be run ad infinitum."], [74.24, 77.077, "They could keep running them and make a so-called infinite forecast,"], [77.077, 80.455, "and generate the statistics of weather, which is what we call climate."], [80.455, 81.915, "So von Neumann wrote a proposal"], [81.915, 83.404, "to perform this infinite forecast."], [83.404, 84.542, "This proposal was approved"], [84.542, 88.713, "by the federal government and became the Geophysical Fluid Dynamics Laboratory,"], [88.755, 91.883, "a NOAA laboratory also located here in Princeton, where I work."], [92.008, 96.429, "How did GFDL evolve into the climate modelling powerhouse it is today?"], [96.429, 99.808, "The first director of GFDL as we know it was Joe Smagorinsky,"], [100.308, 101.684, "who was actually a young scientist"], [101.684, 105.355, "who worked on the computer project here at the Institute with von Neumann."], [105.355, 108.504, "In the mid-1950s, Smagorinsky began to develop a team."], [108.504, 111.069, "They needed to model the sunlight coming in,"], [111.319, 114.28, "the infrared energy going out, the turbulence of the atmosphere,"], [114.28, 116.491, "and also the ocean, because the sunlight warms"], [116.491, 118.201, "the ocean, and the ocean warms the atmosphere."], [118.201, 120.703, "So they brought in experts across these disciplines."], [120.703, 123.123, "And there were two who were the leading figures:"], [123.123, 126.751, "the atmospheric scientist Suki Manabe, and the oceanographer Kirk Bryan."], [126.751, 129.712, "Manabe led the atmospheric model development, and"], [129.712, 131.214, "Bryan the ocean model development."], [131.214, 134.342, "And that was actually the first ocean model of its kind anywhere."], [134.551, 135.218, "And the two of them"], [135.218, 139.472, "collaborated, developing their models separately and also coupling them together"], [139.472, 140.89, "and getting them to talk to each other,"], [140.89, 143.226, "and simulate the Earth system comprehensively."], [143.226, 145.979, "The first version of their model was pretty artificial."], [145.979, 147.939, "It only simulated a sector of the globe,"], [147.939, 150.15, "and the distribution of land was not very realistic,"], [150.15, 152.735, "but they just needed to give it a try and see how it worked."], [152.735, 154.195, "So they built these various prototype models,"], [154.195, 157.532, "and then gradually, over the decades, made them more realistic."], [157.615, 160.869, "What were some famous climate predictions from the 60s and 70s?"], [160.869, 162.495, "And were they accurate?"], [162.495, 165.54, "The first, made in the late 60s, was that the globe would warm"], [165.54, 169.252, "under CO2 by about three degrees per doubling, in equilibrium."], [169.335, 170.461, "And also, surprisingly,"], [170.461, 173.673, "that the stratosphere would cool when we increased carbon dioxide."], [173.673, 176.885, "And so these two forecasts, of global warming and stratospheric cooling,"], [176.926, 181.806, "were made in a 1967 paper published just as GFDL moved to Princeton, New Jersey,"], [182.056, 185.48, "and that's something we've seen in the satellite record since then."], [185.48, 186.144, "In the 1970s,"], [186.144, 189.898, "a more comprehensive version of the model showed that when you double CO2,"], [189.939, 190.773, "the high latitudes"], [190.773, 193.776, "warm faster than the rest of the globe, by about a factor of three."], [193.776, 196.487, "This was a forecast made in a paper in 1975,"], [196.487, 198.907, "and again, over the ensuing decades, that has been true."], [198.907, 202.702, "And papers in the 2000s have shown definitively that the Arctic is warming"], [202.744, 203.703, "faster than the rest of the globe"], [203.703, 206.873, "by about a factor of three, which is about what Manabe predicted."], [206.873, 211.211, "What were some famous climate predictions from the 80s, and were they also accurate?"], [211.211, 213.88, "Throughout the 80s, Manabe and Bryan were working"], [213.88, 217.383, "feverishly on their coupled model, which had realistic bathymetry of the ocean,"], [217.8, 221.638, "and then they made additional discoveries: the land would warm more than the ocean,"], [221.638, 225.642, "by about a factor of a half; and, opposite to the way that the Arctic warms faster"], [225.642, 228.269, "than the rest of the globe, Antarctic warming would be delayed."], [228.269, 232.023, "There is this ocean that encircles Antarctica, and has winds whipping around it"], [232.023, 233.608, "that drive strong ocean currents."], [233.608, 235.235, "The effect of these ocean currents is that"], [235.235, 239.03, "you're drawing up water from the abyss, and it's very hard to warm that water,"], [239.113, 241.355, "because you're always replacing it with new water."], [241.355, 242.7, "That was from a paper in 1989,"], [242.7, 245.161, "and that's in fact just what we see in the observations."], [245.161, 247.872, "How did federal funding make all this possible?"], [247.872, 252.377, "What we're talking about here is decades of stable, government-funded research"], [252.377, 254.771, "that allowed for these climate forecasts."], [254.771, 256.172, "And these things matter."], [256.172, 259.008, "The fact that the Arctic is warming faster than the rest of the globe"], [259.008, 262.095, "means the sea ice is melting, means the northwest passages are opening up,"], [262.095, 266.14, "and that makes the Arctic a more important place geopolitically, and in part explains"], [266.391, 268.559, "why this administration has been interested in Greenland."], [268.559, 271.271, "This is going to be a place where, militarily"], [271.271, 273.606, "and in the search for resources, there is going to be more access,"], [273.606, 276.651, "and that's a direct result of the Arctic amplification of global warming."]], "rob": [[0.291, 1.167, "Hey, I'm Syl."], [1.167, 4.379, "And I'm walking 270 miles from New York City to D.C."], [4.421, 6.756, "in support of weather and climate research."], [6.756, 10.135, "At mile 256, I spoke with Rob Levy."], [10.301, 11.469, "Hi, I'm Rob Levy."], [11.469, 14.431, "I'm a research scientist. I do aerosol remote sensing."], [14.431, 17.6, "What is the history of the MODIS instrument?"], [17.6, 20.061, "The Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer"], [20.061, 23.106, "was launched on a satellite called Terra in 1999."], [23.106, 26.901, "And so my understanding is that it was supposed to be a"], [26.901, 28.319, "five to seven year mission."], [28.319, 30.655, "In the end, the engineering of this particular instrument"], [30.655, 34.034, "and the satellites was so good that they have really lasted for -"], [34.034, 34.909, "I mean, what is it now?"], [34.909, 38.913, "Really, it's been 27 years. There's a second one that was launched a couple of years later."], [38.913, 43.71, "And so between them, it's one of the longest continuous single-instrument"], [43.71, 47.13, "data records. With consistent theory, consistent maths"], [47.13, 48.882, "and consistent retrieval algorithms,"], [48.882, 50.925, "we get something that really shows"], [50.925, 52.427, "how things are changing over time."], [52.427, 55.722, "How do aerosols impact the climate system?"], [55.847, 57.932, "Aerosols have a lot of impacts on a lot of things."], [57.932, 59.768, "One of the major ones is, of course, air quality:"], [59.768, 62.103, "breathing particles like smoke and dust. They also have"], [62.103, 65.857, "a climate impact. Haze reflects light, and that reflection of light,"], [65.857, 69.652, "and maybe absorption of light, changes the radiation in the atmosphere."], [69.652, 72.322, "Has policy changed aerosol emissions over time?"], [72.322, 74.783, "And can you see that in the MODIS data record?"], [74.783, 79.162, "The eastern US is a good place to understand that. The Clean Air Acts really, really worked."], [79.162, 83.249, "You know, I grew up in the DC area, and it was hazy, hot and humid."], [83.249, 88.379, "From these aerosol data records, you can really see that it's cleaner than it was in 2000."]], "ross": [[0.041, 1.084, "Hey, I'm Syl and"], [1.084, 5.52, "I'm walking 270 miles in support of weather and climate research."], [5.52, 6.339, "At mile 255,"], [6.381, 8.03, "I spoke with Ross Salawitch."], [8.03, 9.384, "Hi, I'm Ross Salawitch."], [9.592, 12.178, "I am a professor of atmospheric chemistry"], [12.178, 15.306, "and climate at the University of Maryland, College Park."], [15.306, 18.935, "How does weather and climate research benefit society economically?"], [18.935, 21.104, "For example, here at the University of Maryland,"], [21.104, 23.106, "our air has gotten so much cleaner."], [23.106, 25.859, "Look at this beautiful day,"], [25.859, 27.986, "from the efforts that have been put in place."], [27.986, 31.99, "We study how ozone precursors lead to the bad air we breathe,"], [31.99, 34.659, "how precursors of aerosols lead to particulate matter."], [34.659, 38.371, "And that has huge economic benefit for the health, welfare"], [38.371, 42.208, "and well-being of the citizens of Maryland and throughout the world."], [42.417, 48.006, "How might atmospheric aerosols impact future climate, and what work has your group done on that?"], [48.339, 50.948, "We know that aerosol loading is going down."], [50.948, 52.343, "That will either unmask"], [52.343, 56.347, "a lot of hidden global warming, or perhaps aerosols have been benign."], [56.431, 61.144, "What my group does is come up with probabilistic forecasts of future"], [61.144, 64.522, "warming, because it helps the people who actually make the rules know"], [64.522, 70.111, "how steeply they have to reduce greenhouse gas emissions to achieve policy targets."], [70.195, 74.24, "Why was 2023 to 2024 so remarkably warm?"], [74.24, 77.994, "We have a backdrop of rising human emissions."], [77.994, 79.496, "We had a big ENSO."], [79.496, 83.416, "When ENSO happens, warm water in the west comes east, cuts off"], [83.416, 85.502, "the upwelling, leads to more warmth."], [85.502, 90.757, "We had another very unusual occurrence: the International Maritime Organization"], [90.757, 94.26, "reduced the amount of sulfur in the fuel of the world's ships"], [94.26, 95.501, "in January of 2020."], [95.501, 99.224, "We also saw that the Atlantic Ocean was anomalously warm."], [99.224, 102.519, "And whether or not the Atlantic Ocean warmth"], [102.519, 107.44, "in 23 and 24 was due to this reduction in sulfate emissions is a subject of"], [107.44, 111.319, "active research. The last thing is the Indian Ocean Dipole."], [111.319, 117.2, "What we saw in 23 was very, very warm conditions in the west part of the Indian Ocean."], [117.617, 121.287, "What's your response to the proposed dismantling of NCAR?"], [121.287, 126.042, "There's no rational reason that I can possibly fathom."], [126.042, 129.17, "It would be a giant step backwards"], [129.17, 132.382, "were NCAR to no longer be functional."], [132.465, 136.136, "How have recent federal funding cuts impacted your science?"], [136.136, 137.679, "Right now we're on our last"], [137.679, 143.309, "gasp of funding, with no knowledge of when future opportunities will emerge."]], "tim": [[0.041, 3.962, "Yo, I'm Syl and I'm walking 250 miles from New York City to D.C."], [4.004, 6.339, "in support of weather and climate science."], [6.339, 9.342, "At mile 56, I spoke with Tim Merlis."], [9.634, 13.638, "Hi, my name is Tim Merlis, and I'm a scientist at Princeton"], [13.638, 16.933, "University's Program in Atmospheric and Oceanic Sciences."], [16.933, 20.145, "How do you personally use climate models in your work?"], [20.145, 24.816, "At the university we often take advantage of these incredible tools"], [24.816, 27.819, "that have been built by the government laboratory, to use"], [27.861, 32.449, "in a more scientific sense: to use them to try and understand why"], [32.574, 37.829, "rainfall might increase in some regions, why hurricanes might get more intense."], [38.038, 42.125, "What if, for example, I don't have Earth's continents?"], [42.125, 46.463, "Are there still going to be hurricanes that form in the warm tropical oceans?"], [46.463, 50.884, "I've done research in that mould myself, and the answer to that question is,"], [50.884, 54.554, "if you don't have the continents, you'll have hurricanes forming at basically"], [54.554, 55.593, "every longitude."], [55.593, 57.932, "How have humans changed the climate?"], [57.932, 60.101, "And how have models helped us know?"], [60.101, 62.228, "One thing that we're doing with Earth's climate"], [62.228, 65.69, "is a sort of inadvertent experiment: carbon dioxide"], [65.69, 67.817, "emissions, methane emissions,"], [67.817, 71.571, "other particles being emitted into the atmosphere by human activities."], [71.863, 74.282, "That can cause changes to, say, the temperature."], [74.282, 77.577, "But this is a sort of one-off, inadvertent experiment."], [77.577, 81.414, "With climate models, we can do more focused experiments, and ask"], [81.414, 82.999, "what-if style questions."], [82.999, 86.669, "What if, for example, humans did not emit carbon dioxide?"], [86.669, 90.965, "We can generate this counterfactual Earth climate history."], [91.049, 94.761, "It helps shape policy about what different emissions"], [94.761, 99.307, "levels will do to the climate system, but it can also be a really fruitful tool"], [99.307, 101.017, "for scientific understanding."], [101.017, 102.56, "Have climate models of Earth"], [102.56, 105.772, "been adapted to study other planets in the universe?"], [105.98, 110.11, "Astronomers have been discovering exoplanets, planets around other stars,"], [110.11, 113.696, "and many of them are thought to be in synchronous orbit,"], [113.696, 117.951, "where the sun is always facing the same side of the planet,"], [117.992, 120.995, "much like we're always seeing the same face of the moon."], [120.995, 125.5, "About 15 years ago, we took an Earth climate model, and we moved it"], [125.5, 129.754, "into this exoplanet situation. You might expect the perpetual day"], [129.796, 133.883, "side to be boiling hot and the night side to be freezing cold."], [134.134, 137.804, "Really, those planets may in fact be not too different in terms"], [137.804, 138.972, "of the average temperature"], [138.972, 140.54, "compared to our own planet."], [140.54, 142.225, "Are we alone in the universe?"], [142.225, 144.102, "This, of course, is just one ingredient."], [144.102, 148.898, "That's been a very exciting area of planetary climate science research"], [148.898, 153.653, "that in fact leverages tools that were developed for Earth climate research."]], "aberdeen": [[0, 0.633, "Hey everyone,"], [0.633, 3.333, "welcome to day 10, mile 12,"], [3.333, 5.966, "and I'm on my way down to the University of Maryland."], [6.133, 8.433, "And check out this beautiful road I'm on."], [8.433, 10.666, "It's such a treat, a beautiful dirt road."], [15.866, 18.366, "Howdy. We're 20 miles in on day nine."], [18.366, 21.033, "I'm doing a Reddit AMA with Virginia and Maryam,"], [21.033, 23.1, "who are really helping answer a lot of those questions."], [23.1, 27.733, "And we're cruising on my way to Gunpowder Falls State Park,"], [28.1, 30.966, "which sounds like it could be exciting. We'll see."]], "blog_5_fix": [[0, 1.433, "we're at day five"], [1.433, 3.733, "and I'm on my way down to Temple University"], [3.733, 6.033, "to speak with Rebecca Beadling"], [6.033, 7.4, "who's a climate scientist there"], [7.4, 8.5, "and some of her colleagues"], [15.466, 18.066, "really not the best day to have forgotten my umbrella"], [18.133, 19.233, "10 miles to Temple"], [19.666, 20.3, "alright Andrew"], [20.3, 22.966, "we're at mile 13 on day 5"], [22.966, 24.833, "this is my first time in Philadelphia"], [24.833, 25.7, "I love this place"], [25.8, 27.733, "spirits are high spirits are high"], [28.866, 29.733, "see you at the next update"], [29.733, 30.6, "see you guys"], [30.633, 32.4, "we're at Temple University"], [32.8, 34.2, "that was a long walk that was a long"], [34.2, 35.033, "I need an ice cream"], [35.133, 37.033, "but that's that marks mile a hundred as well"], [37.033, 39.4, "as my GPS is beeping at me"], [39.466, 40.733, "hundred down proud of you man"], [40.733, 42.666, "hundred fifty to go whoo whoo"]], "day_8_full_blog": [[0, 3.066, "We just had an awesome interview with Kelsey Malloy."], [5.9, 7.833, "I had my first cup of coffee,"], [8.133, 9.4, "which was awesome,"], [9.4, 12.966, "so I'm feeling energised on this Saturday morning."], [13.233, 15.166, "Four miles in, day eight."], [15.233, 18.933, "My GPS just said, welcome to Maryland."], [18.933, 20.566, "Seven miles down into day 8,"], [20.566, 22.433, "listening to some Coldplay,"], [22.566, 25.6, "and the trees are releasing some volatile organics,"], [25.6, 30.233, "which smell lovely and are helping form those guys."], [30.266, 32.166, "So life is good. I can't complain."]], "day_10": [[0.166, 3.166, "Day 10, checking in here at mile 18,"], [3.233, 5.433, "on my way down to the University of Maryland."], [5.566, 8.266, "And it's a nice day, passing through Baltimore."], [8.266, 8.933, "Beautiful weather."], [9.166, 9.866, "Peace."], [16.166, 17.833, "So I'm at Long Beach, Maryland now,"], [17.833, 18.633, "30 miles in."], [18.866, 21.366, "I'm gonna go change into some brighter clothes"], [21.366, 23.233, "and try to chip away another twenty."], [32.2, 34.233, "Alright, this is a mile 41 update."], [34.233, 37.066, "It's a beautiful evening - or I guess night,"], [37.066, 39.266, "at this time. It's almost midnight."], [39.3, 41.066, "But the stars were out earlier."], [41.066, 43.8, "It's a little cloudy now, but we survive."], [43.8, 44.466, "We persevere."], [44.466, 46.333, "And then we'll knock out the last 10."], [46.333, 47.966, "What's up, from the University of Maryland."], [48, 50.166, "Mile 50 of the day, here on day 10,"], [50.166, 52.6, "signing off. It's been an awesome day."], [52.7, 55.9, "Beautiful weather, and excited for some interviews here tomorrow,"], [55.9, 60.333, "and then the final descent into DC the following day."], [60.333, 61.966, "So we're really wrapping this up,"], [61.966, 64.2, "and I'm so grateful and so excited."], [64.2, 65.966, "So, see ya."]], "day_11_new": [[0, 2.066, "so excited to be approaching into Washington now"], [2.066, 5.066, "this is so exciting and feeling a lot of gratitude"], [5.066, 8.133, "for everyone who's made this project happen"], [8.133, 9.666, "so that's just incredible"], [9.666, 10.433, "i can't believe it"], [10.433, 14.233, "it feels surreal; I'm excited to go to the capitol tomorrow"], [15.166, 17.266, "final stop day eleven"], [17.266, 19.033, "let's go"], [19.033, 20.5, "thank gosh for smoothies"], [21.266, 22.066, "mm"]], "dc-run": [[11.7, 14.366, "We're on our final stretch here."], [14.566, 16.233, "Ah, I dropped the letters."], [16.666, 19.3, "There we go. They almost went down the grate, but we're good."]], "early_day_3": [[0.7, 1.3, "Hey folks."], [1.3, 2.966, "I'm starting here, day three."], [2.966, 4.966, "I just started at East Brunswick"], [4.966, 6.8, "and I'm a few miles down the road,"], [6.8, 8.6, "and it's a nice day, finally."], [8.6, 10.8, "It's not raining, so I have no complaints."], [21, 23.333, "Excited to meet some folks down at GFDL,"], [23.333, 26.6, "which is Princeton's Geophysical Fluid Dynamics Laboratory."], [26.6, 29.266, "We've got some thin film interference here."], [29.9, 31.766, "Not good for the environment, but beautiful."], [32.966, 33.6, "Oh man."], [33.6, 36.566, "The hardest part seems to be just to keep walking"], [36.566, 38.966, "while I'm listening to Lady Gaga, you know what I mean?"]], "final_step": [[0, 3.3, "now after walking 250 miles from Central Park to Capitol Hill"], [3.3, 4.9, "I would like to invite to the podium"], [4.9, 7.533, "Syl to walk just a few more yards"], [7.866, 11.166, "please come up and officially conclude your walk for science"], [11.2, 12, "yeah"], [12.9, 13.7, "woohoo"], [14.966, 15.766, "yeah Syl"], [18.6, 20.466, "hey everyone can you hear me alright"], [20.466, 21.933, "oh as I smash my mic"], [22.7, 27.366, "so I just walked 270.4 miles to get here today"], [27.366, 28.633, "a little bit more than 250"], [28.633, 32.6, "and I can't feel my legs"], [32.6, 34.7, "but one thing I can feel right here"], [34.7, 38.766, "is all the energy and support of weather and climate research"], [38.766, 40.466, "here in the US yeah yeah"], [43.166, 45.933, "on my walk I talked with Boy Scouts"], [46, 47.6, "firefighters cashiers"], [47.6, 50.4, "hairdressers driveway basketball enthusiasts"], [50.4, 53.566, "you name it I almost sprained my ankle"], [53.666, 56.733, "trying to dunk a basketball on a mini hoop in somewhere New Jersey"], [58.266, 63.1, "I also walked with over 20 climate weather scientists along 12"], [63.5, 66.366, "across 12 institutions on the East Coast"], [67.1, 71.066, "one thing between all the conversations I had remain the same"], [71.633, 75.366, "an appreciation whether we discovered or decades old"], [75.5, 76.6, "of the miracle it is"], [76.6, 79.233, "that we provide such profoundly good weather forecast"], [79.233, 80.1, "here in this country"], [81.166, 85.3, "I walked not only to celebrate this remarkable scientific achievement"], [85.466, 87.9, "but also to bring awareness to the risks"], [87.9, 91.1, "that the agencies that make this research possible face"], [91.433, 93.366, "in light of budget and staffing cuts"], [93.966, 96.133, "I'm speaking for my own personal opinion here"], [97.1, 100.966, "Americans know the value of US weather and climate science"], [101.466, 104.366, "and it's time that these values are reflected in our government"], [104.6, 105.533, "thank you"]], "jonah_full_blog": [[0, 4.8, "Hello. We are currently at day six, mile 0, in downtown Philly."], [4.8, 6.933, "I'm joined by Jonah right now."], [14.766, 17.533, "It is 85 degrees Fahrenheit. Look at that."], [17.533, 19, "Syl, is it humid?"], [19, 21.1, "It's actually relative humidity 27%."], [21.1, 22.5, "Oh, I was wrong."], [22.5, 24, "Weather check done, complete."], [24, 26.2, "Ten miles in. See ya."], [26.2, 29.533, "Won't you take me to Funkytown"], [29.533, 33.066, "Roman cavalry choirs are singing"], [33.066, 36.866, "Oh, living on a prayer"], [36.866, 41.1, "And I would walk 125 miles more"], [41.1, 46.033, "Just to be the person who delivers letters to Congress"], [46.033, 51.466, "About weather and climate research cuts"], [51.466, 52.833, "The clouds are rolling in."], [52.833, 55.1, "I've been learning all about clouds from Syl,"], [55.1, 56.233, "which has been fantastic."], [56.233, 57.8, "And we've been meeting people."], [57.8, 59.266, "Syl handed out some stickers"], [59.266, 60.5, "and had a nice conversation"], [60.5, 62.733, "with the hairdresser of this establishment,"], [62.733, 63.9, "who's been here for -"], [63.9, 66.1, "how many years did you say, Syl?"], [66.1, 66.966, "Thirty-five years."], [83.033, 84.266, "Hey, Lira."]], "molly_cit": [[0.4, 1.233, "Hi everyone."], [1.233, 5.633, "I'm Molly Robey. I'm a PhD student at Johns Hopkins,"], [5.633, 9.133, "as well as a faculty member at Loyola in Baltimore,"], [9.266, 11.1, "and that is where I am walking today."], [11.1, 13.4, "It has finally stopped raining,"], [13.866, 16.066, "but it is so humid outside."], [16.433, 20.6, "I'm just sharing a few tips on how to get involved in research."], [20.833, 23.166, "So, the first one being:"], [23.266, 26, "if you are interested in continuing your education,"], [26, 29.466, "as a graduate student you develop a research question"], [29.6, 33.533, "and then set out to conduct research to answer that question."], [33.566, 36.666, "And that is what I've been doing at Johns Hopkins"], [36.666, 38.733, "over the past three years."], [39, 43.266, "And I will say that the research question is not just one question."], [43.266, 46.266, "It develops over many, many months."], [46.266, 48.2, "My question hasn't been finalised yet."], [48.3, 52.166, "And then a second area is through public service."], [52.233, 55.766, "A lot of the research that I participate in"], [55.833, 60.666, "involves non-profits. So if you are interested in,"], [60.666, 62.466, "let's say, community action,"], [62.466, 66.666, "community engagement - a lot of non-profits are involved in that realm,"], [66.8, 68.6, "and that's how you can get involved in research."], [68.8, 72.466, "And then a third way is through policy."], [72.566, 74.433, "And so, as a researcher,"], [74.433, 78.333, "you can start to connect these dots between a research question,"], [78.633, 80.533, "actually getting out into the community,"], [80.6, 82.433, "and policy. And so"], [82.433, 87.366, "the American Meteorological Society has a science policy colloquium"], [87.6, 91.566, "that young professionals and graduate students can participate in."], [91.633, 94.3, "I had the privilege of doing it last summer."], [94.3, 98.6, "It's a week-long trip to DC, and you get to go to Capitol Hill,"], [98.6, 100.733, "and it really helps you understand"], [101.1, 104.1, "how we can get these research questions started"], [104.166, 107.366, "and then hopefully influence policy down the road."], [107.666, 109.433, "So thanks so much for watching."], [109.433, 114.5, "I hope this helps, and we definitely need to continue our funding"], [114.566, 117, "and our advocacy work in the policy realm."]], "NCA_5": [[0.166, 2.233, "Hello, Weather and Climate Livestream."], [2.233, 4.866, "I am calling from Washington, DC."], [5.166, 6.7, "My name is Allison Crimmins,"], [6.7, 9.966, "and I was the director of the Fifth National Climate Assessment,"], [9.966, 10.818, "or NCA5."], [10.818, 15.933, "The NCA5 is a big report that's super important."], [15.966, 18.566, "It's a congressionally mandated report"], [18.633, 21.3, "that helps us understand the climate risks,"], [21.3, 24.166, "impacts and responses here in the United States."], [24.533, 25.366, "And it's really important,"], [25.366, 27.866, "because it's also written to be very accessible,"], [27.866, 30.2, "with lots of figures and graphs and data,"], [30.2, 32.2, "but also just easy to understand."], [32.2, 34.4, "And it brings climate change home"], [34.4, 36.966, "in a way that's really local for a lot of people."], [37.133, 39.5, "But it's also more than just a report."], [39.6, 42.066, "It's a process that brings together experts"], [42.066, 43.566, "from all around the country,"], [43.833, 47.166, "and these experts volunteer their time and energy"], [47.166, 51.566, "and expertise over multiple years to help draft the assessment."], [51.666, 55.166, "So we're really lucky that we get to learn from all of these experts,"], [55.166, 57.333, "across many different scientific fields."], [57.466, 58.3, "And they come together"], [58.3, 61.6, "to help us understand those climate risks and responses"], [61.6, 65.7, "here in the US. You can access the Fifth National Climate Assessment"], [65.7, 69.133, "at nca5.climate.us."]], "new_jersey_wildlife": [[0.8, 4.033, "So I'm currently passing through South Brunswick Township."], [8.6, 11.233, "It's just absolutely lush and gorgeous here."], [11.233, 14.366, "That's a mile 8 update on day 3,"], [14.466, 16.033, "and I'm on my way to GFDL."], [16.033, 19.2, "I'll be there in, hopefully, two,"], [19.2, 19.966, "three, four hours,"], [19.966, 21.066, "something like that. We'll see."], [26.433, 29.766, "I've gotta thank Crossroads North Middle School in New Jersey here"], [29.766, 34.5, "for a nice little path on my way through to GFDL. Thank you."]], "nyc_to_nj_recap": [[1.1, 2.866, "Alright, I'm almost done with New York."], [2.866, 4.8, "I'm right at the end of Staten Island."], [13.9, 14.7, "Whoo!"], [16.666, 18.166, "That's New Jersey."]], "radnor": [[0.566, 1.166, "Hey y'all."], [1.166, 3.233, "It's day 6 - no,"], [3.233, 7.1, "it's day 7 of walking from New York City to DC,"], [7.166, 9.8, "and I am at Radnor Green Park,"], [9.8, 13.266, "and my hope is to find the trail"], [13.6, 15.733, "on my way down to the University of Delaware."], [23.466, 24.7, "Look at this."], [26.366, 28.166, "This is unbelievable. I found this,"], [28.166, 29, "like, hidden,"], [29, 32.533, "secret little path somewhere in Delaware."], [33.8, 37.7, "Google Maps says it's a public path, so I'm gonna hope it is."]], "raratin_canal_trail_with_andrew": [[0.1, 4.766, "Alright, we're back at mile six with the one and only Andrew Williams,"], [9.3, 12.433, "who many of you may know from the livestream last year."], [12.566, 14.466, "Your twin. My twin."], [14.466, 16.3, "I'm locked in for the marathon today."], [21.266, 23.866, "So tell me about clouds in general."], [23.866, 26.633, "They reflect lots of the sun's radiation back out to space."], [26.633, 27.966, "They help cool the planet."], [28.133, 31.166, "Also, every drop of rain that's ever fallen on your head,"], [31.166, 32.566, "or particularly your head -"], [32.833, 34.233, "What, splashed you? Ew."], [34.233, 35.466, "Ha ha ha. Thank you for the clouds."], [35.533, 39.433, "Modelling clouds is one of the biggest challenges in climate science."], [39.433, 42.666, "It's actually what Syl and I work on as part of our job."], [49.1, 50.5, "If you see a big fluffy cloud"], [50.5, 52.1, "and you can see the edges of it really clearly,"], [52.1, 52.833, "that's probably, like,"], [52.833, 54.133, "more of a water cloud."], [54.133, 56.6, "If you're looking up at the sky and you see these, like,"], [56.6, 58.8, "thin, wispy things that are kind of,"], [58.8, 60.6, "like, smeared out across the sky,"], [60.6, 62.166, "that's probably more of an ice process."], [62.166, 63.033, "Bird!"], [64.566, 65.633, "Bird, bird!"], [66.733, 69.266, "Oh, oh my gosh."], [71.333, 72.333, "Wow."], [77.4, 79.066, "Oh my gosh."], [80.4, 81.933, "That was kind of incredible."]], "sun_ray": [[0, 3.566, "The first ray of sunshine of the trip."], [3.866, 6.266, "Three forty PM, May 25th."], [6.433, 10.2, "So it took a couple of days, but here we go. We got sun."], [10.5, 12.466, "Content buses here to pick me up,"], [12.466, 15.3, "right in front of GFDL. We made it."]], "tim_walk_vibes": [[0.1, 0.866, "Hey everybody."], [1, 2.933, "This is day four, mile one,"], [2.933, 6.433, "and I'm joined by a real life Princeton scientist over here."], [6.433, 8.366, "Tim Merlis. Happy to be here with you, Syl."], [12.9, 15.133, "You know, if you flush the toilet in the northern hemisphere"], [15.133, 16.733, "it's gonna go one way. Yeah."], [16.733, 17.766, "Southern hemisphere, the other."], [17.766, 20.166, "Is that fact or fiction? That's fiction."], [20.4, 24.2, "The timescale on which the Earth is rotating is one day."], [24.233, 26.933, "The time it takes to flush the toilet is super fast."], [27, 28.766, "Yeah, that's too fast"], [28.766, 33.533, "for this slower rotation of the planet to really kick in."], [33.533, 35.533, "So if you have, like, a hurricane,"], [35.533, 38.633, "you're actually feeling these forces, because it's such a large,"], [38.633, 40.433, "slow-scale flow? Exactly."], [40.433, 42.766, "You know, that sort of several-day timescale,"], [42.766, 45.733, "that's the rotation of the planet acting on the storm."], [53.6, 55.9, "We're about to pop out at the pond"], [55.9, 57.733, "where, at the end of the movie Oppenheimer,"], [57.733, 60.433, "you've got Einstein and Oppenheimer having a chat."], [60.433, 62.533, "It's right here on the edge of the pond."], [62.766, 64.966, "Wow, that's pretty cool."], [65, 66.566, "Thanks again for walking with me, Tim."], [66.566, 67.6, "It was such a pleasure."], [67.6, 68.733, "Thanks, Syl, it's awesome."], [68.733, 70.666, "Means a lot. And I'll see you around."], [71.033, 72.433, "Alright, I'll follow along."]], "trenton_bridge": [[0.666, 4.8, "Hello. We are on our way to Philly from New Jersey."], [4.866, 6.3, "Thank you, Trenton."], [6.4, 8.166, "We met some cool people along the way."], [8.166, 9.166, "We're moving to Philly now."], [9.166, 9.866, "Super excited."], [9.866, 11.066, "What's this river? Delaware River?"], [11.066, 12.366, "This is the Delaware River."], [12.366, 13.466, "Delaware River, yeah."], [13.466, 15.5, "We're gonna cross over into Morrisville."], [15.633, 17.233, "See you at the next one. See you guys, cheers."]]}, "clipTitles": {"allegra": "Interview #2 — Allegra LeGrande", "alan": "Interview #3 — Alan Robock", "andra": "Interview #9 — Andra Garner", "becki": "Interview #8 — Becki Beadling", "bernadette": "Interview #6 — Bernadette Woods Placky", "charles": "Interview #11 — Charles Schutte", "hikaru": "Interview #1 — Hikaru Wakeel Hayakawa", "kelsey": "Interview #12 — Kelsey Malloy", "lauren": "Interview #10 — Lauren Kipp", "mitch": "Interview #4 — Mitch Bushuk", "nadir": "Interview #7 — Nadir Jeevanjee", "rob": "Interview #14 — Rob Levy", "ross": "Interview #13 — Ross Salawitch", "tim": "Interview #5 — Tim Merlis", "aberdeen": "Day 9–10 — Aberdeen", "blog_5_fix": "Day 5 — to Temple University", "day_8_full_blog": "Day 8 — into Maryland", "day_10": "Day 10 — the 52-mile day", "day_11_new": "Day 11 — into Washington", "dc-run": "Day 12 — the final sprint", "early_day_3": "Day 3 — East Brunswick", "final_step": "Day 12 — the speech at the Capitol", "jonah_full_blog": "Day 6 — with Jonah", "molly_cit": "Bonus video #2 — Molly Robey", "NCA_5": "Bonus video #1 — Allison Crimmins", "new_jersey_wildlife": "Day 3 — South Brunswick", "nyc_to_nj_recap": "Day 2 — leaving Staten Island", "radnor": "Day 7 — Radnor Green Park", "raratin_canal_trail_with_andrew": "Day 4 — canal trail with Andrew", "sun_ray": "Day 3 — first sunshine at GFDL", "tim_walk_vibes": "Day 4 — walking with Tim Merlis", "trenton_bridge": "Day 4 — crossing the Delaware"}, "langs": {"es": "Español", "fr": "Français", "vi": "Tiếng Việt", "zh": "中文", "mg": "Malagasy"}};
